\par\par \b \uc2 UK Morning Briefing: FTSE Set To Rise; Pound Jumps After BOE Cuts Rates\b0\par\par\uc2 1,535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2020\par 06:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Snapshot: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FTSE to edge higher; GBP/USD 1.1651-53; bund yield -0.192%; 10-year Treasury 1.158%; Brent $29.42; spot gold $1,484.31\f28 \par \par \uc2 Watch For: Public sector finances; Public sector finances; BOE Quarterly Inflation Attitudes Survey, UK banks' external claims statistics; earnings from Wetherspoon's, Manchester & London Investment, BMO Private Equity, \uc2 Faron Pharmaceuticals\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Coronavirus Latest: \par ================== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Italy's Coronavirus Death Toll Overtakes China's\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -EU Relaxes Rules on Government Aid to Companies in Face of Virus\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -U.S. Warns Against International Travel, Urges Citizens Abroad to Return Home\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -U.S. Jobless Claims Rise Sharply at Front End of Expected Coronavirus Surge\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Fed Ramps Up Bond Buying, Indicating Much Larger Purchases Are Likely\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Fed Establishes \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Swap Lines With Nine More Countries\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -McConnell Unveils GOP Stimulus Plan Amid Coronavirus Crisis\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Coronavirus Death Rate in Wuhan Lower Than Initial Estimates, New Study Finds\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -California Orders Lockdown for State's 40 Million Residents \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par Headline News: \par ============= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy's Coronavirus Death Toll Overtakes China's\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italy's death toll from the coronavirus epidemic appears to have overtaken that in China, the latest official data shows, as Chinese fatalities fall sharply and Europe becomes the epicenter of a pandemic that is killing thousands and devastating the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The relentless rise in Italy's virus deaths reflects the particular danger the respiratory disease poses for elderly people in a country that has the world's second-oldest population after Japan\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Thursday afternoon, 3,405 people infected with the coronavirus had died in Italy, an increase of 427 from a day earlier. Some 3,245 people carrying the virus had died in China by the end of Wednesday, a toll that is now rising by only around 10 a day, according to Chinese official data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ---\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Considers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Saudi-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Oil Standoff\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Trump administration is considering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the Saudi-Russian oil-price war, and Texas regulators are weighing whether to curtail crude production for the first time in decades, as U.S. producers suffer from a historic crash in prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Administration officials are exploring a diplomatic push to get the Saudis to cut oil production and threats of sanctions on Russia aimed at stabilizing prices, after U.S. oil companies pressed them to intercede, people familiar with the matter said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ---\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  Relaxes Rules on Government Aid to Companies in Face of Virus\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Thursday agreed to relax the bloc's rules on state aid to companies until December so that governments can mitigate the economic impact of the coronavirus on business.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European governments will be allowed to give grants, tax breaks and advance payments of up to \u8364\'20\'AC800,000 to each company affected by the crisis, said the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  , the bloc's executive, which adopted the new rule after proposing it to member states two days earlier.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ---\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China One-Year Loan Prime Rate Unchanged in March\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's reference lending rates for both short- and long-term loans were unchanged this month, as the country seemingly has the coronavirus crisis mostly under control.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The one-year Loan Prime Rate, a benchmark rate that was revamped in August last year, stood at 4.05% in March, the same as February, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday. The LPR for five-year and longer-term loans was also unchanged at 4.75%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Stocks: \par ====== \par  \par EuroSTOXX50 2482.53   33.50  1.37% \par DAX 30      8708.10   63.00  0.73% \par FTSE 100    5235.50   37.50  0.72% \par Dow        20010.50  -60.00 -0.30% \par S&P 500     2402.70   -6.30 -0.25% \par Nasdaq 100  7331.50   46.20  0.65% \par Futures at 0452 GMT \par  \par Nikkei                      Closed \par Hang Seng   22318.82 609.69  2.81% \par S&P/ASX 200  4869.90  87.00  1.82% \par Shanghai     2714.77  12.64  0.47% \par Shenzhen A   1763.85   2.93  0.17% \par Prices at 0425 GMT \par  \par Dow        20087.19  188.27  0.95% \par Nasdaq       7150.58  160.73  2.30% \par S&P 500     2409.39   11.29  0.47% \par FTSE 100    5151.61   71.03  1.40% \par FTSE 250   12829.70 -178.49 -1.37% \par Xetra DAX   8610.43  168.72  2.00% \par CAC40       3855.50  100.66  2.68% \par STOXX 600    287.80    8.14  2.91% \par EuroSTOXX50 2454.08   68.26  2.86% \par Closing prices \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks looked set for cautious gains at the open after U.S. indexes pushed higher on hopes that government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action can protect the world economy from a painful coronavirus-induced recession.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stock index futures were less than 1% higher Friday as Asian indexes rose, with healthy gains seen in Hong Kong, Seoul and Sydney.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After initially sliding 700 points, the Dow Jones Industrial Average turned sharply higher on Thursday to close up just under 1%. Rallying technology shares helped the Nasdaq Composite gain 2.3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While action from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was reassuring, investors warned that the number of coronavirus cases would still likely drive the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is a market that has nothing to hold onto to steady it," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jeffrey Kleintop\f28 \uc2 \f2  , chief global investment strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Charles Schwab\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "Until we see a peak in cases, we're not likely to see the market bottom." \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ===== \par  \par USD/JPY 110.00-01 -0.64% \par EUR/USD 1.0756-59  0.61% \par GBP/USD 1.1651-53  1.45% \par USD/CNY 7.0862-82 -0.30% \par WSJ Dollar Index 96.15 -0.87% \par US Dollar Index 102.13 -0.61% \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates at 0450 GMT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were higher Friday with the recent strength of the U.S. dollar fading as monetary-policy actions from central banks such as the BOE eased market turmoil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed's establishment of dollar swap lines with nine additional central banks in addition to existing swap lines with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  , BOE, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOJ\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 SNB\f28 \uc2 \f2  is expected to ease USD funding stress and stabilize \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, Maybank said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar, which has rallied strongly in recent sessions as investors have dashed for cash, fell broadly, with the WSJ Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a basket of 16 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  falling 0.9%. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Bonds: \par ===== \par  \par U.S.   10 Year  1.158% \par German 10 Year -0.192% \par Yields at 0447 GMT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. treasury yields ticked higher in Asia, with the yield on the 10-year note rising to 1.158% from 1.121% on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That came after the benchmark bond's yield fell sharply Thursday as investors said asset purchases from the Fed and other global central banks helped to stabilize government bond markets that have been rocked by heavy selling in the last few days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  bought close to $75 billion of Treasurys alone on Thursday. Government bond holders in some eurozone economies like Italy received a lift last night after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced a new EUR750 billion ($811 billion) asset-purchasing program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Fed is very much committed to the old \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mario Draghi\f28 \uc2 \f2  approach of do whatever it takes. At this stage of the game, they're trying to reestablish confidence and order in the markets at the most basic level," said Kathy Jones, chief fixed-income strategist at Schwab Center for Financial Research. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Energy: \par ====== \par  \par Nymex  $26.33 4.40% \par Brent $29.42 3.34% \par Futures at 0449 GMT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices continued to rally in Asia after jumping on Thursday after The Wall Street Journal reported Thursday that the U.S. was considering a diplomatic push to get the Saudis to cut oil production back to levels they had originally signaled.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WTI made its largest one-day percentage gain on record Thursday, jumping 24%, while Brent rose 14%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite Thursday's gains, many say the outlook for oil prices remains bleak. Prices were already falling for much of 2020 due to lagging demand as the coronavirus shut down travel-first in and around China and then world-wide. Oil prices remain down about 60% in 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although there is some hope among traders that a U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might help the Saudis and Russians reach a compromise, part of the day's rise is also being driven by short sellers covering their bets, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Robert Yawger\f28 \uc2 \f2  , director of the futures division at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Securities USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  . "The fundamentals are still very bearish." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Metals: \par ====== \par  \par Spot gold    $1484.31 0.89% \par Gold futures $1485.70 0.43% \par At 0448 GMT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold rose as technical indicators signalled its recent slide was overdone. The stochastic oscillator is still around the 20 threshold, which some traders perceive as oversold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The last 24 hours yielded additional stimulus measures from the Fed, \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  and BOE, which should clearly provide gold a springboard that will shoot prices higher once the scramble for cash eases and dollar loses its crown," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Edward Moya\f28 \uc2 \f2  , senior market analyst at Oanda.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Base-metal prices broadly slid in morning Asia trade. The three-month \f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2  copper contract was down 2.9% at $4,686 a metric ton, while the aluminum contract was 1.0% lower at $1,614 a ton.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to william.horner@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 20, 2020 01:12 ET (05:12 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument D