\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 : New Risk Weights for Russian Banks Amplified by PTI Definition\b0\par\par\uc2 2,043 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2020\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -Moscow/London-09 January 2020: The increase in risk weights for unsecured lending by Russian banks from 1 October 2019 has been amplified by the cautious regulatory definition of payment-to-income (PTI) ratios used in the calculation, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says. Under the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia\uc2 's (CBR) regulatory PTI definition, many more retail borrowers fall into the "PTI above 80%" category, and many fewer fall into the "PTI below 30%" category, than under the borrower distributions based on the banks' own PTI definitions for underwriting purposes.\par \par \uc2 The new risk weights vary according to borrowers' PTI ratios, with higher PTI ratios attracting higher risk weights. The average risk weight for unsecured loans issued in October rose to 220% from 170% for those issued in April-September when the previous risk weights were applicable. While this was largely due to the risk weights themselves, the CBR's conservative PTI definition was an important contributory factor. We estimate that the increase would have been significantly less (30pp rather than 50pp) had banks been allowed to base the risk weights on the PTI ratios that they use for underwriting purposes rather than the CBR's regulatory PTI definition.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/1e9d9712-65e7-4bd4-960d-55544f4c4817?src=embed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Russian Banks: Unsecured Retail Loans by Borrower PTI Ratio}}}\par \par \uc2 We believe that the difference between the distributions reflects the requirement for banks to include only officially confirmed income (or prescribed alternative estimates, which are conservative, if confirmation is not available) when calculating the regulatory PTI ratio. The PTI ratio that banks calculate for underwriting purposes typically includes other income based on estimates or interviews with borrowers: the difference is often significant.\par \par \uc2 The regulatory definition allows banks to use certain alternatives in place of a borrower's officially confirmed income but these also tend to be significantly lower than the borrower's total income. For example, banks may use the average official income for the borrower's region of domicile, but this is usually very low, or they may use 2x the borrower's monthly debt payments to other banks, which implies a 50% PTI at the loan application date before taking account of the new loan.\par \par \uc2 We have found significant variation among banks in their approaches to calculating risk weights for credit cards, which account for about 20% of unsecured lending in Russia. For simplicity, some banks treat all card transactions since 1 October, or even their entire credit card portfolio, as loans exempt from the PTI calculation (allocated to the "n/a" PTI bucket). This leads to risk weights based on a 50%-60% PTI ratio. The "n/a" PTI bucket represented 30% of total unsecured lending at end-October. Others banks calculate individual PTI ratios in certain circumstances, for example when the borrower's monthly balance increases. We expect a more consistent approach to take shape following discussions between banks and the CBR.\par \par \uc2 Banks are discussing with the CBR a more flexible approach to determine PTI ratios and in the medium term they may start using alternative sources to estimate official income where confirmation is unavailable. This could lead to closer alignment with the PTI used for underwriting purposes. However we do not expect the CBR to make significant changes in the near term given that its recent moves have been designed to increase risk weights to curb unsecured lending growth.\par \par \uc2 The CBR has been gradually increasing risk weights on unsecured lending for several years to limit excessive lending to highly leveraged borrowers and the risk of overheating in the banking system. However, these measures have had little impact and we believe loan growth is more likely to be curbed by tighter underwriting in response to deteriorating loan quality than by regulatory \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (See \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 : Loan Quality, Not Regulation May Curb \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Retail Lending.): {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10097714"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.fitchratings.com/site/pr/10097714}}}\par \par \uc2 The quality of retail loans has weakened slightly in recent months, leading to tighter underwriting standards. We believe that this is the main reason for a slight slowdown in the banking sector's retail loan growth in 2H19, although the new risk weights based on PTI ratios also had some effect. Unsecured retail loan growth slowed to 1.3% in October from 1.7% in September.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://e.infogram.com/9e2427a5-12c3-4419-8dc3-29af2eb915a8?src=embed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Russian Banks: Risk Weights for Unsecured Loans from 1 Oct 2019}}}\par \par \uc2 Contact:\par \par \uc2 Ilya Sarzhin\par \par \uc2 Senior Analyst, Financial Institutions - Banks\par \par \uc2 +7 495 956 9983\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings CIS Limited\par \par \uc2 Valovaya Street, 26\par \par \uc2 Moscow 115054\par \par \uc2 Ekaterina Zadonskaya\par \par \uc2 Senior Analyst, Financial Institutions - Banks\par \par \uc2 +7 495 956 5575\par \par \uc2 Ruslan Bulatov\par \par \uc2 Director, Financial Institutions - Banks\par \par \uc2 +7 495 956 9982\par \par \uc2 Anton Lopatin\par \par \uc2 Director, Financial Institutions - Banks\par \par \uc2 +7 495 956 7096\par \par