\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2018\par 13:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1119 GMT - Turkey is witnessing a vicious cycle of a weaker lira undermining confidence amongst investors, as reflected in the sharp fall in Turkish stocks and bonds, says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  . "This in turn could trigger substantial capital outflows and lead to even stronger selling pressure on the lira," it says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stop or at least slow this vicious cycle or at least can slow it down by raising interest rates on April 25. However, this "would be an unpopular decision amongst prominent Turkish officials, who are in favour of preserving robust economic growth ahead of crucial elections next year," says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  . USD/TRY last up 1.4% at 4.1687, having hit a record high of 4.1735, according to Factset.(yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1048 GMT - Some media reports that the U.S. may be thinking of imposing further sanctions on Russia after the chemical attack in Syria prompt \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  to warn of a "risk of further ruble depreciation." USD/RUB continues to fall on Wednesday, with USD/RUB up 1.5% at 64.0937, having reached its highest in more than 16 months at 64.7350, according to Factset. "In the current uncertain situation, we think the RUB will likely remain under pressure," \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. The ruble has dropped to a string of multi-month lows this week.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1022 GMT - \uc2 HSBC\uc2  shifts to "mildly bullish" from "neutral" on inflation-linked bonds, says rates strategist Theologis Chapsalis. Carry turns positive for both eurozone and U.K. linkers in April, "so investors are enticed to increase their inflation-linked allocations." Italy and France have already responded to this demand by launching new inflation-linked bonds in the last week of March. Chapsalis says the mix of higher headline HICPx [HICP ex-tobacco] and softer core inflation is "particularly favorable" for eurolinkers as it leads to higher carry while providing ammunition for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be dovish. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1008 GMT - With the Russian ruble continuing to fall, there is a "small ray of hope for the RUB," which could "come from oil prices after Saudi voiced its support to keep brent crude closer to $80 a barrel in 2019," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . However, "it is too soon to call the all-clear" in the Russian ruble downturn until there is greater certainty over how the U.S. will respond to the chemical attack in Syria. The current levels of the Russian ruble are "attractive" though, SocGen says. USD/RUB last up 1.7% at 64.2572, having reached its highest since late 2016 at 64.7350, according to Factset.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0953 GMT - The Russian ruble continues to fall on Wednesday, with USD/RUB up 1.2% at 63.9460, but until USD/RUB gets closer to 75, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . "This is still around 16% away from the current spot level, and it would require large institutional outflows to get there," SocGen says. But "large flows have not yet been identified," SocGen adds. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0942 GMT - As the euro rises after \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  member \uc2 Ewald Nowotny\uc2  said he had no problem with increasing the deposit rate from -0.4% to -0.2%, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  reiterates that the euro appreciation is "fragile," keeping its forecast of "downside pressure" on EUR/USD over the coming months. "The more the market is concentrating on EUR positive news right now, the higher the potential for disappointments for the euro if the \uc2 ECB\uc2  back paddles," it says. EUR/USD is last up 0.1% to 1.2370, partially due to some dollar weakness. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT [Dow Jones ] Currently trading at 138.82, the Italian June BTP contract is under pressure and stands below its 50-period moving average at 138.90 on a 30-min chart. Moreover, the intraday RSI stands within its selling area (between 50 and 30) and confirms the bearish bias. A first target to the downside is therefore set at Apr. 3 top at 138.45. A break below this threshold would trigger a bearish acceleration towards the horizontal support and overlap at 138.20 and at 137.92 in extension. Only a rebound above the horizontal resistance at 139.00 would turn the intraday outlook to bullish with a first alternative target set at the horizontal resistance at 139.26 and a second one set at Apr. 5 top at 139.43 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones ] Currently trading at GBP 0.8722, the euro is on the upside, above its 50-period moving average at GBP 0.8713 on a 30-min chart. Furthermore, the intraday RSI stands within its buying area between 50 and 70 and confirms the bullish bias. Thus, as long as the horizontal support at GBP 0.8705 holds on the downside, further advance is likely towards the strong horizontal resistance at GBP 0.8725 and towards the horizontal resistance at GBP 0.8740 in extension. A third target is set at Apr. 6 top at GBP 0.8750. Only a break below the horizontal support at GBP 0.8705 would turn the outlook to bearish with a first alternative target set at Apr. 10 bottom at GBP 0.8690 and a second one set at the horizontal support at GBP 0.8675 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0905 GMT - Taiwan's inflationary pressure is on the rise and together with an improving economy it will create a window of opportunity for normalizing monetary policy, says \uc2 BofA Merrill Lynch\uc2 . March inflation eased to 1.6% from a 2.2% seasonal spike in February, but still pushed up the average for the year so far. The bank expects the pace of price growth will more than double from last year's average of 0.6%. Still, the potential spread of US trade protectionism and strained cross-strait relations could pose headwinds to the exports sector and lower the probability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raising rates this year, it adds.(chester.yung@wsj.com;@chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT - The U.K. industrial production rose by 0.1% month-on-month in February, a much weaker increase compared with January and compared with the consensus in a WSJ poll. Sterling falls only slightly on the news, given that further data showed the U.K. trade deficit shrank to GBP10.2 billion in February. GBP/USD falls slightly to 1.4195 from 1.4209 beforehand, but this still leaves it up by 0.1% and not far from an earlier two-week high of 1.4224. EUR/GBP rises to 0.8718, from 0.8703 previously. Manufacturing production fell 0.2% in February, compared with a 0.1% rise in the previous month. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0849 GMT - U.K. factory output declined in February, the latest sign the economy slowed in the first quarter. Manufacturing output fell 0.2%, though overall industrial production recorded a 0.1% gain, thanks largely to higher energy generation as Britons nudged up the thermostat during a cold snap, the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  says. Most forecasters expect the U.K. to lag its peers for a second year during 2018 as uncertainty over the country's future ties to the \uc2 European Union\uc2  weigh on activity. (Jason.Douglas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 GMT - Turkey's current account deficit data in February were slightly better than expectations but still significantly higher year-on-year, taking the 12-month rolling deficit to $53.3 billion, close to 6% of GDP, says Tim Ash of \uc2 Bluebay Asset Management\uc2  . He notes that portfolio flows saw outflows for February of $227 million, after a net inflow of $5 billion in January. He says the reversal of portfolio inflows is a concern, as they have been funding half the current account deficit. If they reverse this could spell double trouble for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "This release just affirms the vulnerabilities on the BOP [balance of payments] front for Turkey, and underlines the challenges for the \uc2 CBRT\uc2  in managing the lira when Erdogan has tied both hands behind its back in terms of limiting its ability to hike policy rates," Ash says. USD/TRY is last up 0.6% at 4.1437. (yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2018 07:19 ET (11:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180411ee4b001a4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
