\par\par \b \uc2 Talking Markets: Russian Ruble Falls Show No Sign of Abating\b0\par\par\uc2 By Olga Cotaga \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2018\par 17:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble fell for a third consecutive day on Wednesday after Washington imposed a second wave of sanctions on Russian oligarchs and companies associated with them, and analysts see little prospect of the falls slowing.\par \par \uc2 Tweets by U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on Wednesday saying U.S. missiles "will be coming" to Syria and that U.S.-Russian relations were worse than ever exacerbated the fall in the ruble.\par \par \uc2 This situation leaves investors concerned the sanctions could be extended to other Russian companies and assets.\par \par \uc2 "The Russian ruble depreciation and the massive sell-off in the Russian stock market came on the back of the U.S. announcement of its latest sanctions against Russia, which has affected many non-sanctioned companies in the same way as the sanctioned ones because investors fear the recoupling effects," said \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  analysts.\par \par \uc2 The ruble reached its lowest level against the dollar since November 2016, with USD/RUB rising as high as 65.0359, according to Factset. It also fell to a two-year low against the euro, with EUR/RUB reaching 80.4986.\par \par \uc2 "In the current uncertain situation, we think the ruble will likely remain under pressure," \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 With the ruble falling, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to try and stabilize prices. But most investors say it is too early to think of such a possibility.\par \par \uc2 "We do not think the \uc2 CBR\uc2  will be panicked into \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8230\'20\'26until USD/RUB is much closer to 75," said analysts from \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The country used a lot of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves back in 2014, when Russia first got hit with sanctions for the annexation of Crimea.\par \par \uc2 "After the heavy drain ... it seems unlikely that the authorities would want to be particularly active in markets," BNY Mellon analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Still, high oil prices and capital flows could help cushion the ruble's falls.\par \par \uc2 "Russian ruble high carry status and likely capital repatriation by major Russian businessmen, oligarchs and wealthy individuals may offset or, at least, ease the negative pressure from financial investors selling the ruble now," ING analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Although the ruble's falls have been quite substantial, the drop "so far looks rather contained."\par \par \uc2 USD/RUB now looks "closer to the levels which we assessed in December 2017 as fair, given the current oil prices and the fiscal rule," ING said.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, analysts warn of some ominous signs.\par \par \uc2 BNY Mellon warns that trouble in Russian markets could herald trouble in global markets, because ruble volatility "has often coincided with broader global market unrest."\par \par \uc2 "If you take the history of the last 20 years where there has been unrest on Russian markets it has fed through into broader uncertainty," says Simon Derrick, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon.\par \par \uc2 ING also warned that the impact of Russian sanctions could turn out to be deeper than they look initially.\par \par \uc2 "The direct impact on the Russian economy through the sanctioned entities doesn't look too biting ... but non-U.S. entities may start behaving in a way that poses additional restrictions not stated in the official sanction wording."\par \par \uc2 "Together with the defending strategies used by global banks' compliance/legal departments, this creates another layer of self-made sanctions from global banks and financial firms."\par \par \uc2 Write to Olga Cotaga at olga.cotaga@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2018 11:44 ET (15:44 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180411ee4b002de\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
