\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Latest on Trump, U.S. Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 1,335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2018\par 14:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and U.S. politics. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0850 ET - US benchmark oil prices jump toward multi-year highs above $66/bbl after President Trump signals a plan to fire missiles into Syria. "Russia vows to shoot down any and all missiles fired at Syria," Trump begins in a morning \uc2 Twitter\uc2  message. "Get ready Russia, because they will be coming." The Tweet immediately sparked heavy buying of crude oil. The looming possibility the US pulls out of an Iran nuclear deal and reimposes sanctions is also lending bullish support. Meantime, traders will watch for weekly US crude oil inventory data at 10:30am ET, after private API late Tuesday reported a bearish rise in its data released. WTI rises 1% to $66.16/bbl. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 ET - \uc2 US government\uc2  bond prices rose after data showed consumer prices fell in March, easing investor concerns that inflation could be picking up. The yield on the benchmark 10-year \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  note was recently at 2.765%, compared with 2.799% Tuesday. Yields came under pressure earlier as threats from President Trump to Russia that US missiles "will be coming" to Syria push US stock futures lower. (akane.otani@wsj.com; @akaneotani)\par \par \uc2 0815 ET - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be less willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the fall of the Russian ruble after the "heavy drain" in Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves during the 2014 crisis, according to BNY Mellon. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of a new fiscal rule, is currently buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the extra cash from oil revenues. Moreover, BNY Mellon says that "Economy Minister \uc2 Maxim Oreshkin\uc2  implied exactly...that the floating RUB would help to mitigate the impact of new U.S. sanctions on the Russian economy." USD/RUB last up 2.3% at 64.6068, EUR/RUB up 2.5% at 80.0004.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0813 ET - The fall in the Russian ruble is likely to continue, given that USD/RUB is "being used as a hedge vehicle by bond investors," who until now have been mostly long the Russian ruble, says ING. Falls could be offset by capital flows, however. "RUB high carry status and likely capital repatriation by major Russian businessmen, oligarchs and wealthy individuals may offset or, at least, ease the negative pressure from financial investors selling RUB now," ING says.USD/RUB rises 2.3% to its highest in more than 16 months at 65.0359, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0805 ET - The latest sanctions on Russia have created a "precedent which could be used against any Russian company without clear and transparent arguments," says ING. This increases the risks of further sanctions, it says. "Investors may try to limit exposure to Russian stocks and bonds" as a result. "The same arguments work for foreign banks working in Russia--mostly European, not the U.S. banks--exposing them to potential losses via direct profit and loss effects, and a loss of future business," ING adds. USD/RUB is last up 2.2% at 64.5641. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0754 ET - The indirect effects of U.S. sanctions on Russian oligarchs and their public companies may be more important, given that the direct impact of the actual sanctions on the Russian economy "doesn't look too biting," says ING. "Non-U.S. entities may start behaving in a way that poses additional restrictions not stated in the official sanction wording." There may appear some "defending strategies used by global banks' compliance/legal departments," which would create another layer of self-made sanctions from global banks and financial firms, ING says. The fall in the Russian ruble continues for a third consecutive day, with USD/RUB rising to its highest in more than 16 months at 64.0359 and EUR/RUB reaching a 2-year high of 80.4986, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0648 ET - Some media reports that the U.S. may be thinking of imposing further sanctions on Russia after the chemical attack in Syria prompt \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  to warn of a "risk of further ruble depreciation." USD/RUB continues to fall on Wednesday, with USD/RUB up 1.5% at 64.0937, having reached its highest in more than 16 months at 64.7350, according to Factset. "In the current uncertain situation, we think the RUB will likely remain under pressure," \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. The ruble has dropped to a string of multi-month lows this week.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0608 ET - With the Russian ruble continuing to fall, there is a "small ray of hope for the RUB," which could "come from oil prices after Saudi voiced its support to keep brent crude closer to $80 a barrel in 2019," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . However, "it is too soon to call the all-clear" in the Russian ruble downturn until there is greater certainty over how the U.S. will respond to the chemical attack in Syria. The current levels of the Russian ruble are "attractive" though, SocGen says. USD/RUB last up 1.7% at 64.2572, having reached its highest since late 2016 at 64.7350, according to Factset.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0421 ET - The former leader of the \uc2 WTO\uc2  doesn't get Trump on trade. His views are "very different" from what most people believe in, \uc2 Pascal Lamy\uc2  contended at the \uc2 Boao Forum\uc2  in Hainan. He added the President believes at the same time that imports to the US are bad while exports are great. "To be frank, I don't understand what is the purpose of Mr. Trump. I doubt whether we live on the same trade planet." (lin.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0243 ET - The FTSE 100 is set to fall in opening deals as jitters about a trade war and potential air strikes on Syria dampen sentiment. London's blue-chip index is tipped to drop 24 points lower to 7242 after markets digest comments by Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  about opening up the country's economy. "What President Xi actually said wasn't much different to his previous speeches," says Michael Hewson at \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  . "For equity markets to regain a sense of equilibrium, we need to start to see progress on the road away from a potential trade war, and currently there's no evidence of that." On the data front, attention is set to focus on February U.K. industrial and manufacturing production data. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1736 ET - \uc2 General Mills\uc2  ' non-executive board member \uc2 Brad Anderson\uc2  stepped down from his post after reports surfaced that a political group he donated to contributed to anti-Muslim ads in the 2016 presidential campaign. Anderson, who is the former CEO of \uc2 Best Buy\uc2 , said in statements that he wasn't aware the group was anti-Muslim. He has also stepped down from several other boards of directors, including the Mayo Clinic, in recent days. (annie.gasparro@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1508 ET - \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  is on the Senate hot seat but \uc2 Apple\uc2  has already been dragged into the fray. During Senator's opening remarks, \uc2 Apple\uc2  was mentioned among the tech companies that collect data on users. The mention is likely to generate some angst in Cupertino. \uc2 Apple\uc2  has tried to draw a distinction between its approach to data collection and tech peers like \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  . It often disassociates data from users and keeps voice data from Siri for less time than peers. But the mention of \uc2 Apple\uc2  in opening remarks shows those distinctions can be lost in a broad look at data collection without regard for how it's done or how the data is used. (tripp.mickle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2018 08:50 ET (12:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180411ee4b001sp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
