\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2018\par 14:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0813 ET - Quarterly U.K. GDP growth slowed to 0.2% in the first quarter of 2018 from 0.4% in 4Q, following weaker-than-expected U.K. industrial production data, says the National Institute of Economic and Social Research (NIESR). "The main reason for the weakness was severe weather in March, which is likely to have disrupted activity," though poor weather can sometimes result in data revisions, says \uc2 Amit Kara\uc2  , head of U.K. macroeconomic forecasting at NIESR. According to earlier figures, U.K. industrial output rose 0.1% on month in February. Sterling turns lower on the day against the dollar, last at 1.4165, down from 1.4196 before NIESR's announcement. EUR/GBP up 0.2% at 0.8739.\par \par \uc2 (jessica.fleetham@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0813 ET - The fall in the Russian ruble is likely to continue, given that USD/RUB is "being used as a hedge vehicle by bond investors," who until now have been mostly long the Russian ruble, says ING. Falls could be offset by capital flows, however. "RUB high carry status and likely capital repatriation by major Russian businessmen, oligarchs and wealthy individuals may offset or, at least, ease the negative pressure from financial investors selling RUB now," ING says.USD/RUB rises 2.3% to its highest in more than 16 months at 65.0359, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0805 ET - The latest sanctions on Russia have created a "precedent which could be used against any Russian company without clear and transparent arguments," says ING. This increases the risks of further sanctions, it says. "Investors may try to limit exposure to Russian stocks and bonds" as a result. "The same arguments work for foreign banks working in Russia--mostly European, not the U.S. banks--exposing them to potential losses via direct profit and loss effects, and a loss of future business," ING adds. USD/RUB is last up 2.2% at 64.5641. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0754 ET - The indirect effects of U.S. sanctions on Russian oligarchs and their public companies may be more important, given that the direct impact of the actual sanctions on the Russian economy "doesn't look too biting," says ING. "Non-U.S. entities may start behaving in a way that poses additional restrictions not stated in the official sanction wording." There may appear some "defending strategies used by global banks' compliance/legal departments," which would create another layer of self-made sanctions from global banks and financial firms, ING says. The fall in the Russian ruble continues for a third consecutive day, with USD/RUB rising to its highest in more than 16 months at 64.0359 and EUR/RUB reaching a 2-year high of 80.4986, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0725 ET - \uc2 HSBC\uc2  remains "mildly bearish" on core eurozone bonds, like Germany, anticipating falling prices and rising yields. This view is based on the prospect of an end to the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's net asset purchases pulling 10-year core yields higher, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  rates strategist Wilson Chin. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  maintains its call for a year-end level in 10-year German bund yields of 0.90%, despite the recent fall in core rates, Chin says. The 10-year Bund yield last trades at a yield of 0.49%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0719 ET - Turkey is witnessing a vicious cycle of a weaker lira undermining confidence amongst investors, as reflected in the sharp fall in Turkish stocks and bonds, says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  . "This in turn could trigger substantial capital outflows and lead to even stronger selling pressure on the lira," it says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could stop or at least slow this vicious cycle or at least can slow it down by raising interest rates on April 25. However, this "would be an unpopular decision amongst prominent Turkish officials, who are in favour of preserving robust economic growth ahead of crucial elections next year," says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  . USD/TRY last up 1.4% at 4.1687, having hit a record high of 4.1735, according to Factset.(yeliz.candemir@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 ET - Some media reports that the U.S. may be thinking of imposing further sanctions on Russia after the chemical attack in Syria prompt \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  to warn of a "risk of further ruble depreciation." USD/RUB continues to fall on Wednesday, with USD/RUB up 1.5% at 64.0937, having reached its highest in more than 16 months at 64.7350, according to Factset. "In the current uncertain situation, we think the RUB will likely remain under pressure," \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. The ruble has dropped to a string of multi-month lows this week.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0622 ET - \uc2 HSBC\uc2  shifts to "mildly bullish" from "neutral" on inflation-linked bonds, says rates strategist Theologis Chapsalis. Carry turns positive for both eurozone and U.K. linkers in April, "so investors are enticed to increase their inflation-linked allocations." Italy and France have already responded to this demand by launching new inflation-linked bonds in the last week of March. Chapsalis says the mix of higher headline HICPx [HICP ex-tobacco] and softer core inflation is "particularly favorable" for eurolinkers as it leads to higher carry while providing ammunition for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be dovish. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0608 ET - With the Russian ruble continuing to fall, there is a "small ray of hope for the RUB," which could "come from oil prices after Saudi voiced its support to keep brent crude closer to $80 a barrel in 2019," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . However, "it is too soon to call the all-clear" in the Russian ruble downturn until there is greater certainty over how the U.S. will respond to the chemical attack in Syria. The current levels of the Russian ruble are "attractive" though, SocGen says. USD/RUB last up 1.7% at 64.2572, having reached its highest since late 2016 at 64.7350, according to Factset.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0553 ET - The Russian ruble continues to fall on Wednesday, with USD/RUB up 1.2% at 63.9460, but until USD/RUB gets closer to 75, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . "This is still around 16% away from the current spot level, and it would require large institutional outflows to get there," SocGen says. But "large flows have not yet been identified," SocGen adds. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0542 ET - As the euro rises after \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  member \uc2 Ewald Nowotny\uc2  said he had no problem with increasing the deposit rate from -0.4% to -0.2%, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  reiterates that the euro appreciation is "fragile," keeping its forecast of "downside pressure" on EUR/USD over the coming months. "The more the market is concentrating on EUR positive news right now, the higher the potential for disappointments for the euro if the \uc2 ECB\uc2  back paddles," it says. EUR/USD is last up 0.1% to 1.2370, partially due to some dollar weakness. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0526 ET [Dow Jones ] Currently trading at 138.82, the Italian June BTP contract is under pressure and stands below its 50-period moving average at 138.90 on a 30-min chart. Moreover, the intraday RSI stands within its selling area (between 50 and 30) and confirms the bearish bias. A first target to the downside is therefore set at Apr. 3 top at 138.45. A break below this threshold would trigger a bearish acceleration towards the horizontal support and overlap at 138.20 and at 137.92 in extension. Only a rebound above the horizontal resistance at 139.00 would turn the intraday outlook to bullish with a first alternative target set at the horizontal resistance at 139.26 and a second one set at Apr. 5 top at 139.43 in extension. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2018 08:13 ET (12:13 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180411ee4b001j2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
