\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Latest on Trump, U.S. Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 1,390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2018\par 18:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and U.S. politics. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1243 ET - Despite settlement discussions described as "swift" by special master Kenneth Feinberg, \uc2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles\uc2  remains months away from reaching deal with US and California officials over more than 100K diesel-powered trucks and SUVs that allegedly violate legal limits on pollution. \uc2 FCA\uc2  is "a number of months from having signed documents with the government," outside lawyer for company Robert Giuffra told US district judge during a hearing in San Francisco. Government lawyers echoed his sentiments. Justice Department sued \uc2 FCA\uc2  on behalf of \uc2 EPA\uc2  , accusing auto maker of using illegal software similar to kind \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  installed to cheat on government emissions tests. Case affects 2014-2016 Jeep Grand Cherokee SUVs and Ram pickup trucks with diesel engines. Settlement with owners of affected vehicles looms once deal reached between \uc2 FCA\uc2  and government officials. (mike.spector@wsj.com; @MikeSpectorWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1116 ET - Shares of energy companies jump, posting the biggest gains of the S&P 500's 11 sectors, as fears of escalating tensions in the Middle East send crude oil prices higher. The S&P 500 energy sector was recently up 0.8%, bucking the broader S&P 500's 0.5% decline, while U.S. crude oil rose 2.6% to $67.18 a barrel--extending gains from earlier in the session when President Trump said to Russia on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that missiles "will be coming" to Syria. (akane.otani@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1024 ET - Wall Street remains as bullish as last year on a potential Sprint/\uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  tie-up, though there's some head-scratching about timing. \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  notes \uc2 Sprint\uc2  's announced plans to plow billions of dollars into its network likely lit a fire under talks, since the more \uc2 Sprint\uc2  invests now, the lower the value of joining forces becomes. The political calendar also adds haste. The current administration's been "less merger-friendly than many would have anticipated," research firm MoffettNathanson says. "But the odds of merger approval under a Democratic administration would almost certainly be worse." (andrew.fitzgerald@wsj.com; @drewfitzgerald\par \par \uc2 0923 ET - BNY Mellon advises keeping a close eye on Russian stock and bond markets and the Russian ruble after U.S. imposed new sanctions, because "volatility in the RUB has often coincided with broader global market unrest." The ruble wasn't always the causal factor, but "it certainly was a significant influence both in 1998," when the Russian government devalued the ruble, and in late 2014, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would allow the ruble to trade freely. "If you take the history of the last 20 years where there has been unrest on Russian markets it has fed through into broader uncertainty," says Simon Derrick, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon. USD/RUB is last up 2.1% at 64.4905. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0850 ET - US benchmark oil prices jump toward multi-year highs above $66/bbl after President Trump signals a plan to fire missiles into Syria. "Russia vows to shoot down any and all missiles fired at Syria," Trump begins in a morning \uc2 Twitter\uc2  message. "Get ready Russia, because they will be coming." The Tweet immediately sparked heavy buying of crude oil. The looming possibility the US pulls out of an Iran nuclear deal and reimposes sanctions is also lending bullish support. Meantime, traders will watch for weekly US crude oil inventory data at 10:30am ET, after private API late Tuesday reported a bearish rise in its data released. WTI rises 1% to $66.16/bbl. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 ET - \uc2 US government\uc2  bond prices rose after data showed consumer prices fell in March, easing investor concerns that inflation could be picking up. The yield on the benchmark 10-year \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  note was recently at 2.765%, compared with 2.799% Tuesday. Yields came under pressure earlier as threats from President Trump to Russia that US missiles "will be coming" to Syria push US stock futures lower. (akane.otani@wsj.com; @akaneotani)\par \par \uc2 0815 ET - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be less willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the fall of the Russian ruble after the "heavy drain" in Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves during the 2014 crisis, according to BNY Mellon. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of a new fiscal rule, is currently buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the extra cash from oil revenues. Moreover, BNY Mellon says that "Economy Minister \uc2 Maxim Oreshkin\uc2  implied exactly...that the floating RUB would help to mitigate the impact of new U.S. sanctions on the Russian economy." USD/RUB last up 2.3% at 64.6068, EUR/RUB up 2.5% at 80.0004.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0813 ET - The fall in the Russian ruble is likely to continue, given that USD/RUB is "being used as a hedge vehicle by bond investors," who until now have been mostly long the Russian ruble, says ING. Falls could be offset by capital flows, however. "RUB high carry status and likely capital repatriation by major Russian businessmen, oligarchs and wealthy individuals may offset or, at least, ease the negative pressure from financial investors selling RUB now," ING says.USD/RUB rises 2.3% to its highest in more than 16 months at 65.0359, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0805 ET - The latest sanctions on Russia have created a "precedent which could be used against any Russian company without clear and transparent arguments," says ING. This increases the risks of further sanctions, it says. "Investors may try to limit exposure to Russian stocks and bonds" as a result. "The same arguments work for foreign banks working in Russia--mostly European, not the U.S. banks--exposing them to potential losses via direct profit and loss effects, and a loss of future business," ING adds. USD/RUB is last up 2.2% at 64.5641. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0754 ET - The indirect effects of U.S. sanctions on Russian oligarchs and their public companies may be more important, given that the direct impact of the actual sanctions on the Russian economy "doesn't look too biting," says ING. "Non-U.S. entities may start behaving in a way that poses additional restrictions not stated in the official sanction wording." There may appear some "defending strategies used by global banks' compliance/legal departments," which would create another layer of self-made sanctions from global banks and financial firms, ING says. The fall in the Russian ruble continues for a third consecutive day, with USD/RUB rising to its highest in more than 16 months at 64.0359 and EUR/RUB reaching a 2-year high of 80.4986, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0648 ET - Some media reports that the U.S. may be thinking of imposing further sanctions on Russia after the chemical attack in Syria prompt \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  to warn of a "risk of further ruble depreciation." USD/RUB continues to fall on Wednesday, with USD/RUB up 1.5% at 64.0937, having reached its highest in more than 16 months at 64.7350, according to Factset. "In the current uncertain situation, we think the RUB will likely remain under pressure," \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. The ruble has dropped to a string of multi-month lows this week.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0608 ET - With the Russian ruble continuing to fall, there is a "small ray of hope for the RUB," which could "come from oil prices after Saudi voiced its support to keep brent crude closer to $80 a barrel in 2019," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . However, "it is too soon to call the all-clear" in the Russian ruble downturn until there is greater certainty over how the U.S. will respond to the chemical attack in Syria. The current levels of the Russian ruble are "attractive" though, SocGen says. USD/RUB last up 1.7% at 64.2572, having reached its highest since late 2016 at 64.7350, according to Factset.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2018 12:43 ET (16:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180411ee4b002li\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
