\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Latest on Trump, U.S. Politics\b0\par\par\uc2 1,285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2018\par 20:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and U.S. politics. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1409 ET - Fed officials meeting in March said they planned to continue raising interest rates as the economy strengthens, according to meeting minutes released Wednesday. "All participants saw some further firming of the stance of monetary policy as likely to be warranted," the minutes said. Some even said they now believed it would be appropriate to raise rates more than previously anticipated, the minutes said. Officials raised interest rates by a quarter-percentage point in March and penciled in two more rate increases this year. (david.harrison@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1408 ET - A stronger economy and firming inflation made Fed officials more confident they would achieve their 2% target, according to minutes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's March meeting released Wednesday. Officials said the upbeat economic data "had increased the likelihood of progress toward the committee's 2% inflation objective," the minutes said. The Fed's preferred inflation measure has undershot the goal for much of the past five years but has been strengthening over the past few months. (david.harrison@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1407 ET - Federal Reserve officials meeting in March worried that a trade war could hurt the US economy, according to minutes of the meeting released Wednesday. Although officials did not expect the Trump administration's announced tariffs on imported steel and aluminium to have a big effect on the outlook by themselves, they nonetheless expressed concerns about escalating the trade dispute. "A strong majority of participants viewed the prospect of retaliatory trade actions by other countries, as well as other issues and uncertainty associated with trade policies, as downside risks for the US economy," the minutes said. (david.harrison@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1307 ET - The Russian ruble reversed earlier losses against the dollar after Fox News reported that satellite images showed Russian warships leaving a Syrian port. The dollar was recently down 0.6% against the ruble, after being up more than 2% earlier in the session. Earlier President Trump warned that US missiles "will be coming" to Syria, signaling a willingness to challenge Russia directly in launching a military strike over an alleged chemical-weapons attack. (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1243 ET - Despite settlement discussions described as "swift" by special master Kenneth Feinberg, \uc2 Fiat Chrysler Automobiles\uc2  remains months away from reaching deal with US and California officials over more than 100K diesel-powered trucks and SUVs that allegedly violate legal limits on pollution. \uc2 FCA\uc2  is "a number of months from having signed documents with the government," outside lawyer for company Robert Giuffra told US district judge during a hearing in San Francisco. Government lawyers echoed his sentiments. Justice Department sued \uc2 FCA\uc2  on behalf of \uc2 EPA\uc2  , accusing auto maker of using illegal software similar to kind \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  installed to cheat on government emissions tests. Case affects 2014-2016 Jeep Grand Cherokee SUVs and Ram pickup trucks with diesel engines. Settlement with owners of affected vehicles looms once deal reached between \uc2 FCA\uc2  and government officials. (mike.spector@wsj.com; @MikeSpectorWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1116 ET - Shares of energy companies jump, posting the biggest gains of the S&P 500's 11 sectors, as fears of escalating tensions in the Middle East send crude oil prices higher. The S&P 500 energy sector was recently up 0.8%, bucking the broader S&P 500's 0.5% decline, while U.S. crude oil rose 2.6% to $67.18 a barrel--extending gains from earlier in the session when President Trump said to Russia on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that missiles "will be coming" to Syria. (akane.otani@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1024 ET - Wall Street remains as bullish as last year on a potential Sprint/\uc2 T-Mobile\uc2  tie-up, though there's some head-scratching about timing. \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  notes \uc2 Sprint\uc2  's announced plans to plow billions of dollars into its network likely lit a fire under talks, since the more \uc2 Sprint\uc2  invests now, the lower the value of joining forces becomes. The political calendar also adds haste. The current administration's been "less merger-friendly than many would have anticipated," research firm MoffettNathanson says. "But the odds of merger approval under a Democratic administration would almost certainly be worse." (andrew.fitzgerald@wsj.com; @drewfitzgerald\par \par \uc2 0923 ET - BNY Mellon advises keeping a close eye on Russian stock and bond markets and the Russian ruble after U.S. imposed new sanctions, because "volatility in the RUB has often coincided with broader global market unrest." The ruble wasn't always the causal factor, but "it certainly was a significant influence both in 1998," when the Russian government devalued the ruble, and in late 2014, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would allow the ruble to trade freely. "If you take the history of the last 20 years where there has been unrest on Russian markets it has fed through into broader uncertainty," says Simon Derrick, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon. USD/RUB is last up 2.1% at 64.4905. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0850 ET - US benchmark oil prices jump toward multi-year highs above $66/bbl after President Trump signals a plan to fire missiles into Syria. "Russia vows to shoot down any and all missiles fired at Syria," Trump begins in a morning \uc2 Twitter\uc2  message. "Get ready Russia, because they will be coming." The Tweet immediately sparked heavy buying of crude oil. The looming possibility the US pulls out of an Iran nuclear deal and reimposes sanctions is also lending bullish support. Meantime, traders will watch for weekly US crude oil inventory data at 10:30am ET, after private API late Tuesday reported a bearish rise in its data released. WTI rises 1% to $66.16/bbl. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 ET - \uc2 US government\uc2  bond prices rose after data showed consumer prices fell in March, easing investor concerns that inflation could be picking up. The yield on the benchmark 10-year \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  note was recently at 2.765%, compared with 2.799% Tuesday. Yields came under pressure earlier as threats from President Trump to Russia that US missiles "will be coming" to Syria push US stock futures lower. (akane.otani@wsj.com; @akaneotani)\par \par \uc2 0815 ET - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be less willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the fall of the Russian ruble after the "heavy drain" in Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves during the 2014 crisis, according to BNY Mellon. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of a new fiscal rule, is currently buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the extra cash from oil revenues. Moreover, BNY Mellon says that "Economy Minister \uc2 Maxim Oreshkin\uc2  implied exactly...that the floating RUB would help to mitigate the impact of new U.S. sanctions on the Russian economy." USD/RUB last up 2.3% at 64.6068, EUR/RUB up 2.5% at 80.0004.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0813 ET - The fall in the Russian ruble is likely to continue, given that USD/RUB is "being used as a hedge vehicle by bond investors," who until now have been mostly long the Russian ruble, says ING. Falls could be offset by capital flows, however. "RUB high carry status and likely capital repatriation by major Russian businessmen, oligarchs and wealthy individuals may offset or, at least, ease the negative pressure from financial investors selling RUB now," ING says.USD/RUB rises 2.3% to its highest in more than 16 months at 65.0359, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2018 14:09 ET (18:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180411ee4b002rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
