\par\par \b \uc2 Market Talk Roundup: Russian Ruble Extends Losses\b0\par\par\uc2 998 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2018\par 15:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 For a third day running the Russan ruble fell sharply on Wednesday, reaching its weakest levels in more than 16 months against the dollar and in two years against the euro. Many analysts forecast more weakness to come, but they don't expect the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take action to stem these losses, at least for now. Some point to mitigating factors for the ruble, including high oil prices and its appeal in carry trades. The following is a selection of market moves and analyst comments.\par \par \uc2 0953 GMT - The Russian ruble continues to fall on Wednesday, with USD/RUB up 1.2% at 63.9460, but until USD/RUB gets closer to 75, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "This is still around 16% away from the current spot level, and it would require large institutional outflows to get there," SocGen says. But "large flows have not yet been identified," SocGen adds. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1008 GMT - With the Russian ruble continuing to fall, there is a "small ray of hope for the RUB," which could "come from oil prices after Saudi voiced its support to keep brent crude closer to $80 a barrel in 2019," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . However, "it is too soon to call the all-clear" in the Russian ruble downturn until there is greater certainty over how the U.S. will respond to the chemical attack in Syria. The current levels of the Russian ruble are "attractive" though, SocGen says. USD/RUB last up 1.7% at 64.2572, having reached its highest since late 2016 at 64.7350, according to Factset.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1048 GMT - Some media reports that the U.S. may be thinking of imposing further sanctions on Russia after the chemical attack in Syria prompt \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  to warn of a "risk of further ruble depreciation." USD/RUB continues to fall on Wednesday, with USD/RUB up 1.5% at 64.0937, having reached its highest in more than 16 months at 64.7350, according to Factset. "In the current uncertain situation, we think the RUB will likely remain under pressure," \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. The ruble has dropped to a string of multi-month lows this week.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1154 GMT - The indirect effects of U.S. sanctions on Russian oligarchs and their public companies may be more important, given that the direct impact of the actual sanctions on the Russian economy "doesn't look too biting," says ING. "Non-U.S. entities may start behaving in a way that poses additional restrictions not stated in the official sanction wording." There may appear some "defending strategies used by global banks' compliance/legal departments," which would create another layer of self-made sanctions from global banks and financial firms, ING says. The fall in the Russian ruble continues for a third consecutive day, with USD/RUB rising to its highest in more than 16 months at 64.0359 and EUR/RUB reaching a 2-year high of 80.4986, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1205 GMT - The latest sanctions on Russia have created a "precedent which could be used against any Russian company without clear and transparent arguments," says ING. This increases the risks of further sanctions, it says. "Investors may try to limit exposure to Russian stocks and bonds" as a result. "The same arguments work for foreign banks working in Russia--mostly European, not the U.S. banks--exposing them to potential losses via direct profit and loss effects, and a loss of future business," ING adds. USD/RUB is last up 2.2% at 64.5641. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1213 GMT - The fall in the Russian ruble is likely to continue, given that USD/RUB is "being used as a hedge vehicle by bond investors," who until now have been mostly long the Russian ruble, says ING. Falls could be offset by capital flows, however. "RUB high carry status and likely capital repatriation by major Russian businessmen, oligarchs and wealthy individuals may offset or, at least, ease the negative pressure from financial investors selling RUB now," ING says.USD/RUB rises 2.3% to its highest in more than 16 months at 65.0359, according to Factset. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1215 GMT - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be less willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the fall of the Russian ruble after the "heavy drain" in Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves during the 2014 crisis, according to BNY Mellon. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as part of a new fiscal rule, is currently buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the extra cash from oil revenues. Moreover, BNY Mellon says that "Economy Minister \uc2 Maxim Oreshkin\uc2  implied exactly...that the floating RUB would help to mitigate the impact of new U.S. sanctions on the Russian economy." USD/RUB last up 2.3% at 64.6068, EUR/RUB up 2.5% at 80.0004.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1323 GMT - BNY Mellon advises keeping a close eye on Russian stock and bond markets and the Russian ruble after U.S. imposed new sanctions, because "volatility in the RUB has often coincided with broader global market unrest." The ruble wasn't always the causal factor, but "it certainly was a significant influence both in 1998," when the Russian government devalued the ruble, and in late 2014, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would allow the ruble to trade freely. "If you take the history of the last 20 years where there has been unrest on Russian markets it has fed through into broader uncertainty," says Simon Derrick, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon. USD/RUB is last up 2.1% at 64.4905. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 11, 2018 09:38 ET (13:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180411ee4b0020q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
