\par\par \b \uc2 EXPLAINER-Latvia's reputation at stake after corruption allegations\b0\par\par\uc2 980 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2018\par 21:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By John O'Donnell and Gederts Gelzis\par     RIGA, Feb 21 (Reuters) - Latvia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief has\par been suspended pending an investigation into bribery\par allegations, and the Baltic country's third biggest bank has\par sought emergency support following U.S. accusations of\par money-laundering and sanctions-busting.\par     The problems, which the government says are not connected,\par have dealt a blow to Latvia's reputation as a financial hub that\par bridges Russia, which it borders, and the West.  \par     \par     WHAT IS THE CRISIS ABOUT?\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Department\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Financial Crimes Enforcement\par Network said on Feb. 13 it was seeking restrictions on ABLV bank\par on suspicion of involvement in money laundering and helping \par clients violate United Nations sanctions on North Korea.\par              \par     ABLV denied the accusations. But it said on Monday it had\par sought temporary liquidity support from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after\par depositors withdrew 600 million euros ($740 million), about 22\par percent of total deposits, following the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  comments.\par             \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  has stopped all payments at the bank to prevent its\par collapse.\par     Separately, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ilmars Rimsevics\f28 \uc2 \f2  was detained\par on Saturday on suspicion that he solicited a bribe. He was\par released on Monday, and denied the accusations, but was\par suspended from his post on Tuesday.\par     Latvia's Defence Ministry said on Tuesday the allegations\par may be part of a disinformation campaign intended to damage\par trust in the country and influence the outcome of a\par parliamentary election in October.\par     It did not say who was behind this but drew parallels with\par campaigns before recent French, German and U.S. elections.\par Russia has denied it was behind those campaigns and says it does\par not meddle in elections in the West.             \par    \par     WHO IS THE SUSPENDED \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  CHIEF?\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ilmars Rimsevics\f28 \uc2 \f2  was part of the political movement that led\par Latvia to independence from the Soviet Union and is a member of\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governing council. \par     The 52-year-old economist is known internationally as a\par conservative policy maker and in Latvia as a firm manager at the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to people who know him.\par     Rimsevics says he is the victim of a smear campaign and\par refuses to resign. He is not allowed leave the country. \par     "I have not demanded or received any bribes," Rimsevics told\par a news conference on Tuesday. "I have become the target of some\par Latvian commercial banks to destroy Latvia's reputation."\par     \par     WHAT IS AT STAKE?\par     Latvia has a population of only about 2 million. Occupied by\par the Soviet Union during World War Two, it won independence in\par 1990, joined the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  in 2004 and adopted the euro\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a decade later.\par     The allegations against Rimsevics and ABLV could damage\par Latvia's standing in the euro zone and its reputation as a\par financial hub, Latvian politicians say.\par     The events of the past few days have shaken Latvia's\par financial system but economists say it would survive intact if\par ABLV collapsed and see little likelihood of contagion into other\par Baltic states.\par     Latvia's economy has suffered much more serious financial\par problems before. Its economy nosedived during the 2008-09 global\par financial crisis that forced it to seek an international rescue,\par but recovered after the adoption of austerity measures including\par slashing spending and wages and increasing taxes.\par           \par     DO LATVIA'S PROBLEMS AFFECT THE \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 ?\par     Latvia's commercial banking association called on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  on\par Wednesday to step in and sort out the problems at ABLV, putting\par pressure on it to explain why it has not acted in the crisis.\par             \par     What has happened in Latvia raises questions about a system\par that makes the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  responsible for supervising banks across the\par euro zone, including many in Latvia\par     One senior official at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  said it had been caught by\par surprise by the developments in Latvia and that the governing\par council was informed by conference call only at the weekend. The\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has since remained publicly silent.             \par     But the root cause of the problem was hardly hidden.\par Latvia's banks hold around $13 billion worth of foreign\par deposits, roughly half of all deposits in the sector, an issue\par highlighted as a source of risk by authorities year after year. \par     \par     IS MONEY LAUNDERING A PROBLEM IN LATVIA?\par     International authorities have long worried about small\par banks in Latvia that hold funds for foreign clients, mostly from\par  Russia and other former Soviet republics. Several of the\par clients have been punished for breaches of rules on\par money-laundering and financing of terrorism, and for sanctions\par busting.             \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in 2007 that Latvia was\par hosted many shell companies and had a reputation "for widespread\par lax due diligence" in the opening of bank accounts.  \par     The situation had improved little when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Organisation for\par Economic Co-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Development\f28 \uc2 \f2  criticised Latvia in 2015\par for failing to do enough to tackle financial crime, including\par the "laundering of proceeds of corruption from overseas".\par     Although it conceded that Latvia had sought international\par cooperation to tackle corruption, it urged stronger anti-money\par laundering measures and warned of the risk that foreign bribes\par could be laundered by foreigners through Latvian banks.\par     \par     DOES RUSSIA HAVE A ROLE IN THE CRISIS? \par     Latvia's relationship with Russia has long been complicated\par but has deteriorated since Russia seized the Crimea peninsula\par from Ukraine in 2014 and fighting broke out in east Ukraine\par between government forces and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -backed separatists.\par     Many Latvians saw \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Crimea as a\par worrying precedent. Their country not only borders Russia but\par has the largest Russian-speaking minority in the Baltics.\par     Asked about suggestions that Russia may have had a role in\par the crisis, Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said "such\par statements are not measured and lack a sense of reality." \par  \par ($1 = 0.8120 euros)\par \par  (Additional reporting by Simon Johnson in Stockholm and Balazs\par Koranyi in Frankfurt, Editing by Timothy Heritage)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-2-21T21:00:35.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180221ee2l00x6q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
