\par\par \b \uc2 Latvia's reputation at stake after corruption allegations\b0\par\par\uc2 970 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Februar 2018\par 21:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RIGA (Reuters) - Latvia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief has been suspended pending an investigation into bribery allegations, and the Baltic country's third biggest bank has sought emergency support following U.S. accusations of money-laundering and sanctions-busting.\par \par \uc2 The problems, which the government says are not connected, have dealt a blow to Latvia's reputation as a financial hub that bridges Russia, which it borders, and the West.\par \par \uc2 WHAT IS THE CRISIS ABOUT?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2 's Financial Crimes Enforcement Network said on Feb. 13 it was seeking restrictions on ABLV bank on suspicion of involvement in money laundering and helping clients violate United Nations sanctions on North Korea.\par \par \uc2 ABLV denied the accusations. But it said on Monday it had sought temporary liquidity support from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after depositors withdrew 600 million euros ($740 million), about 22 percent of total deposits, following the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  comments.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ECB\uc2  has stopped all payments at the bank to prevent its collapse.\par \par \uc2 Separately, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Ilmars Rimsevics\uc2  was detained on Saturday on suspicion that he solicited a bribe. He was released on Monday, and denied the accusations, but was suspended from his post on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Latvia's Defence Ministry said on Tuesday the allegations may be part of a disinformation campaign intended to damage trust in the country and influence the outcome of a parliamentary election in October.\par \par \uc2 It did not say who was behind this but drew parallels with campaigns before recent French, German and U.S. elections. Russia has denied it was behind those campaigns and says it does not meddle in elections in the West.\par \par \uc2 WHO IS THE SUSPENDED \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  CHIEF?\par \par \uc2 Ilmars Rimsevics\uc2  was part of the political movement that led Latvia to independence from the Soviet Union and is a member of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governing council.\par \par \uc2 The 52-year-old economist is known internationally as a conservative policy maker and in Latvia as a firm manager at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to people who know him.\par \par \uc2 Rimsevics says he is the victim of a smear campaign and refuses to resign. He is not allowed leave the country.\par \par \uc2 "I have not demanded or received any bribes," Rimsevics told a news conference on Tuesday. "I have become the target of some Latvian commercial banks to destroy Latvia's reputation."\par \par \uc2 WHAT IS AT STAKE?\par \par \uc2 Latvia has a population of only about 2 million. Occupied by the Soviet Union during World War Two, it won independence in 1990, joined the \uc2 European Union\uc2  in 2004 and adopted the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a decade later.\par \par \uc2 The allegations against Rimsevics and ABLV could damage Latvia's standing in the euro zone and its reputation as a financial hub, Latvian politicians say.\par \par \uc2 The events of the past few days have shaken Latvia's financial system but economists say it would survive intact if ABLV collapsed and see little likelihood of contagion into other Baltic states.\par \par \uc2 Latvia's economy has suffered much more serious financial problems before. Its economy nosedived during the 2008-09 global financial crisis that forced it to seek an international rescue, but recovered after the adoption of austerity measures including slashing spending and wages and increasing taxes.\par \par \uc2 DO LATVIA'S PROBLEMS AFFECT THE \uc2 ECB\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Latvia's commercial banking association called on the \uc2 ECB\uc2  on Wednesday to step in and sort out the problems at ABLV, putting pressure on it to explain why it has not acted in the crisis.\par \par \uc2 What has happened in Latvia raises questions about a system that makes the \uc2 ECB\uc2  responsible for supervising banks across the euro zone, including many in Latvia\par \par \uc2 One senior official at the \uc2 ECB\uc2  said it had been caught by surprise by the developments in Latvia and that the governing council was informed by conference call only at the weekend. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since remained publicly silent.\par \par \uc2 But the root cause of the problem was hardly hidden. Latvia's banks hold around $13 billion worth of foreign deposits, roughly half of all deposits in the sector, an issue highlighted as a source of risk by authorities year after year.\par \par \uc2 IS MONEY LAUNDERING A PROBLEM IN LATVIA?\par \par \uc2 International authorities have long worried about small banks in Latvia that hold funds for foreign clients, mostly from Russia and other former Soviet republics. Several of the clients have been punished for breaches of rules on money-laundering and financing of terrorism, and for sanctions busting.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said in 2007 that Latvia was hosted many shell companies and had a reputation "for widespread lax due diligence" in the opening of bank accounts.\par \par \uc2 The situation had improved little when the \uc2 Organisation for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development\uc2  criticized Latvia in 2015 for failing to do enough to tackle financial crime, including the "laundering of proceeds of corruption from overseas".\par \par \uc2 Although it conceded that Latvia had sought international cooperation to tackle corruption, it urged stronger anti-money laundering measures and warned of the risk that foreign bribes could be laundered by foreigners through Latvian banks.\par \par \uc2 DOES RUSSIA HAVE A ROLE IN THE CRISIS?\par \par \uc2 Latvia's relationship with Russia has long been complicated but has deteriorated since Russia seized the Crimea peninsula from Ukraine in 2014 and fighting broke out in east Ukraine between government forces and \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 -backed separatists.\par \par \uc2 Many Latvians saw \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Crimea as a worrying precedent. Their country not only borders Russia but has the largest Russian-speaking minority in the Baltics.\par \par \uc2 Asked about suggestions that Russia may have had a role in the crisis, Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov said "such statements are not measured and lack a sense of reality."\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 0.8120 euros)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Simon Johnson in Stockholm and Balazs Koranyi in Frankfurt, Editing by Timothy Heritage) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-2-21T20:20:49.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180221ee2l00y11\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
