\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Russia's rising rating comes with sanctions stress\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2018\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats story from Sunday)\par \par \uc2 * Chances of Russia regaining investment grade status rising\par \par \uc2 * Would pave the way inclusion into crucial bond indexes\par \par \uc2 * U.S. sanctions could pose a problem for bond buyers\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 11 (Reuters) - Russia's chances of reclaiming the 'investment grade' credit rating it lost three years ago are rising, potentially causing headaches for index-tracking bond buyers who will be wary of U.S. sanctions.\par \par \uc2 A move by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  late last month means that both it and S&P Global now have Russia's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt rating just one step away from the coveted upper bracket.\par \par \uc2 Targeted by Western economic and financial sanctions since 2014, after it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Ukraine, \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy began to recover last year after a steep economic downturn, thanks to higher oil prices and some prudent measures by the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With that pick-up ongoing, one of the lowest debt levels in the world at just over 16 percent of GDP, plus nearly half a trillion dollars in reserves, many argue the reinstatement of Russia's 'investment grade' status is long overdue.\par \par \uc2 "Our analysis is that the economy and public finances are in good shape and can withstand new sanctions," Moody's analyst \uc2 Kristin Lindow\uc2  told Reuters last week.\par \par \uc2 That was before the United States published a list of oligarchs close to the Kremlin and what sanctions may follow is as yet unknown.\par \par \uc2 But S&P also has Russia on its upgrade watch list and expects to make a decision this year. Its next review is due by mid-March.\par \par \uc2 Ratings upgrades would not only show Moscow is coping with Western sanctions, but also make its bonds of high enough quality for the super-sized pension funds and more conservative exchange-traded funds that only hold 'investment grade' assets.\par \par \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures show that foreign buying of the country's dollar and other non-rouble debt started to pick up last year having been in an almost continual decline since 2011. But a restored investment grade could really rev it up.\par \par \uc2 "It is a big deal crossing the investment grade threshold," said David Riley, head of credit strategy for BlueBay asset and former top sovereign analyst at another ratings agency, Fitch.\par \par \uc2 "It broadens and deepens the investor base and opens the door to some of the (investment grade-only) aggregate bond indices."\par \par \uc2 For instance, the Bloomberg Barclay's Global Aggregate index - which Russian foreign debt will join if the country regains investment grade - acts as a performance benchmark for funds managing trillions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 That influences buying behaviour, because as any money manager knows, if you stray too far from the benchmark then you better be right.\par \par \uc2 "I would say that once it (investment grade) happens, a good number of investors that are benchmarked to those IG indices would buy," said Allianz Global Investor's CIO of EM fixed income Greg Saichin.\par \par \uc2 PANDORA'S BOX\par \par \uc2 More demand for its debt should bring real benefits to Moscow.\par \par \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  analysts calculate that for every 1 percentage point increase in foreign ownership of an emerging market country's debt, a government's yields - effectively what it pays to borrow - drop by about 12 basis points on average.\par \par \uc2 Russia's situation certainly isn't straightforward, though.\par \par \uc2 What if the indexes give the green light to Russia's inclusion and then the United States slaps sanctions on its debt, as has been talked about in Washington recently?\par \par \uc2 That could potentially expose global funds, which are some the biggest customers of both the index providers and S&P and \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , to legal headaches.\par \par \uc2 Abhishek Kumar, lead EM portfolio manager at State Street Global Advisors, who expects a Russian upgrade this year, thinks this worry has been one of the things that has been holding the agencies back over the last year.\par \par \uc2 "It will open a Pandora's box for their clients," Kumar said. "You will have a massive issue: what happens? Do you buy? do you not buy?"\par \par \uc2 BlueBay's Riley agreed. "It is a material issue any investor has to be aware of," he said.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Sujata Rao in London and Katya Golubka in Moscow; editing by Giles Elgood) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-2-12T08:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180212ee2c00ach\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
