\par\par \b \uc2 NORDIC MORNING BRIEFING: Swedish Manufacturing Data in Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dominic Chopping \par \uc2 1,231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2016\par 04:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR - All times GMT \par  \par Nordic Macro \par 0730 Sweden 2Q Industry Capacity \par             Utilization \par  \par Other Equities \par Deere & Co 3Q \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Estee Lauder Companies Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  4Q \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Foot Locker Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2  2Q \par  \par GLOBHAL NEWS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco President \f28 \uc2 \f2 John Williams\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Thursday that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should move to raise interest rates "sooner rather than later," siding with a growing chorus of Fed officials who believe the economy is strong enough to tolerate higher borrowing costs and worry about overshooting the Fed's inflation target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Mr. Williams described a healthy economy with on-pace jobs growth, strong consumer spending and improving household balance sheets. He also said there are no signs that the U.K.'s decision to exit the \uc2 European Union\uc2  will throw the U.S. economy off course.\par \par \uc2 German tax revenues fell by 1.9% in July year-over-year, the first decline since May 2014, the finance ministry said in its latest monthly report. The decline is a result of special circumstances, as many retailers had stocked up on cigarette packages in the month before, ahead of the introduction of obligatory shock photos on the packaging in July, the ministry said.\par \par \uc2 The ministry also said the economy is in good shape and growth should continue in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Russia is bolstering its military presence on its western border, sending tens of thousands of soldiers to newly built installations within easy striking distance of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The moves, which come as Moscow ratchets up confrontation over the Black Sea peninsula of Crimea, are a centerpiece of a new military strategy the Kremlin says is meant to counter perceived threats from the \uc2 North Atlantic Treaty Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Military analysts say the deployments appear to be an effort to build a more permanent and robust military posture around Ukraine, where \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has carried out covert military \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at maintaining influence in its West-leaning neighbor. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IN FOCUS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is a very thin calendar globally Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The earnings season in the Nordics has drawn to a close and the only economic data due is Swedish manufacturing capacity utilization and inventories for the second quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEB said the National Institute of Economic Research survey indicates capacity utilization is trending gradually higher and Friday's data will give clues to possible revisions to the second-quarter flash GDP.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "NIER survey indicates that manufacturing inventories increased significantly in Q2. According to the flash GDP estimate inventories contributed only moderately to Q2 growth. Strong business sector production also imply that the inventories build up was strong[er] than indicated by flash GDP." \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par  \par EUR/NOK \par 0150 GMT 9.2608-58 \par Previous 9.2536-86 \par %Chg +0.08 \par  \par EUR/SEK \par 0150 GMT 9.4714-814 \par Previous 9.4710-810 \par %Chg +0.00 \par  \par USD/NOK \par 0150 GMT 8.1673-733 \par Previous 8.1484-544 \par %Chg +0.23 \par  \par USD/SEK \par 0150 GMT 8.3541-631 \par Previous 8.3418-508 \par %Chg +0.15 \par  \par NORDIC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In SEB's view, one of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Norges Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's most important factors to consider when setting rates is the krone exchange rate, so a quick rally in the NOK similar to last week will most likely draw attention from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It said that while NOK developments aren't yet alarming, it might become a bigger issue should EUR/NOK push towards and below 9.10, as it would increase the chance of a rate cut at the upcoming Sept. 22 decision.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With our positive medium-term NOK view intact, we recommend to trade the 9.10-9.40 range in EUR/NOK in the short-term." \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCK INDEXES: \par  \par OMXN40 0.96% \par OMXS30 0.79% \par OBX    0.51% \par OMXC20 1.36% \par OMXH25 1.14% \par  \par Brent crude: $50.78 \par  \par NORDIC MARKETS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nordic stock markets closed higher Thursday. European shares gained as the market pushed the FOMC rate-hike cycle further down the curve, following Wednesday night's FOMC minutes, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Saxo Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The main beneficiaries were miners and commodity names, as Brent was testing the $50/barrel level partly on the back of lower dollar."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vestas Wind Systems\f28 \uc2 \f2  were the biggest blue-chip gainer on the day, hitting 8-year highs after stellar earnings and the launch of a buyback program. In oil, Brent crude concluded its journey back to $50/barrel after receiving a boost from the price-bullish U.S. inventory report Wednesday, Saxo added. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent prices were shedding gains in early Asia trade Friday, but the prospect of major producers, including Russia and Saudi Arabia, banding together in the coming months to stabilize output was boosting sentiment, even though skepticism around the possibility is growing as well.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent rose above $50 for the first time in six weeks on Thursday and has managed to stay above the threshold. The strengthening dollar, however, might provide some headwinds. Brent was down 11 cents at $50.78 a barrel, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  was up 3 cents at $48.25 a barrel. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEWS ROUNDUP: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DENMARK: Denmark's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vestas Wind Systems AS\f28 \uc2 \f2  (VWS.KO), one of the world's largest makers of wind turbines, posted a strong rise in second-quarter net profit thanks to higher deliveries in the U.S. and Germany. The company said net profit more than doubled to 278 million euros ($313.4 million) in the three months to June 30, from EUR125 million in the same period a year earlier. Revenue grew 46% to EUR2.56 billion from EUR1.75 billion. Vestas is now expanding again, with U.S. deliveries growing 45% on the year to 779 megawatts, partly due to the country's extension last year of a key tax credit until 2023, with a gradual reduction as of next year. Vestas shares soared on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2  Copenhagen Thursday on the back of the strong second-quarter earnings and the launch of share buyback plan. The company said it intended to spend 2.98 billion Danish kroner to buy its own shares thanks to a recent jump in cash flow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWEDEN: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Volvo Car Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and ride-hailing giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Uber Technologies Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . have signed a $300 million agreement to co-develop autonomous sport-utility vehicles that will either be used as self-driving taxis or sold to consumers. The two companies will announce the joint project Thursday, saying the Swedish auto maker will conduct much of the initial engineering on a platform for an XC90 SUV that is capable of piloting itself. At some point, Uber will take that technology and use it as the foundation to develop its own self-driving vehicle technology. Volvo will do the same. Under the agreement, Uber will buy XC90 SUVs manufactured by Volvo. Uber, known for its ride-hailing service, has been developing autonomous vehicles and envisions a day when those cars could replace its tens of thousands of contract drivers. It recruited a team of researchers and scientists from Carnegie Mellon University, hired the hackers who wirelessly took control of a Jeep in 2015, and earlier this year hired \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ford\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Sherif Marakby to head its global vehicle programs. Uber and Volvo will work together on dynamic mapping, object identification and other hurdles that face many companies working on driverless cars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Dominic Chopping at dominic.chopping@wsj.com; Twitter: @domchopping @WSJNordics\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 18, 2016 22:35 ET (02:35 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160819ec8j0002d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
