\par\par \b \uc2 Syria's war-battered pound hit by Russian withdrawal\b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2016\par 16:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Iranian transfers have prevented even steeper slide\par \par \uc2 * Trade has been "dollarised"\par \par \uc2 * Rates vary across patchwork of territories\par \par \uc2 By Suleiman Al-Khalidi\par \par \uc2 March 18 (Reuters) - Battered by war which has inflicted incalculable damage on industry, infrastructure and economy, Syria's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit new lows this week after Russia said it was reducing its military support to President Bashar al-Assad.\par \par \uc2 The Syrian pound has fallen to 475 to the dollar on the black market, a 90 percent drop since March 18, 2011, when security forces fired on protesters in the city of Deraa, sparking an uprising which descended into civil war.\par \par \uc2 Backed by financial and trade support from Iran, Syria's government succeeded in stabilising the pound early in the conflict.\par \par \uc2 But the slide accelerated as it lost control of territory and border crossings, trade collapsed, Western sanctions bit, Gulf Arab investment dried up, major cities were devastated and half the population was displaced.\par \par \uc2 The collapse of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has driven up inflation and aggravated wartime hardship, as Syrians struggle to afford basics such as food and power. Government budget spending in pounds has more than doubled, but in dollar terms has crashed.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September turned the tide of war in Assad's favour, but only briefly stemmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline, and Moscow's declaration on Monday that it was pulling forces out of the country hit the pound again.\par \par \uc2 PANIC\par \par \uc2 "In the last few days it came under further pressure because of the Russian announcement," a Damascus-based businessman said. "There was a lot of panic".\par \par \uc2 At the start of the uprising, the pound was around 47 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Today the (official \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate is around 406 but it reached 475 pounds in the black market," Hani al-Khoury, a financial consultant based in Damascus, said late on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 He said official efforts had prevented an even graver depreciation.\par \par \uc2 "Compared to the extent of the crisis and its devastating impact on the economy, the pound could have been far more affected," he told Reuters by telephone.\par \par \uc2 Infusions of money from Iran and dollar remittances from Syrians working abroad have also helped prevent an even steeper freefall, bankers say.\par \par \uc2 Iran is believed to have deposited hundreds of millions of dollars in the country's depleted reserves.\par \par \uc2 The government has also clamped down on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchanges in an effort to narrow the gap between official and black market rates. But in recent weeks the official rate has fallen as fast as the black market, showing the limits of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  influence.\par \par \uc2 DOLLARISATION\par \par \uc2 The pound's fall has pushed Syrian traders to switch their financing increasingly into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Khoury said, a trend which may have been accelerated by the flows of billions of dollars of international humanitarian aid into the country.\par \par \uc2 "The Syrian economy has been dollarised - the import side and the financing side, along with savings and the aid coming from outside," he said.\par \par \uc2 That, combined with the continued pressures caused by the war, meant that whatever steps authorities take, the pound "is bound to continue to gradually drop".\par \par \uc2 Militant jihadists control most of the east of the country, rebels and Islamist fighters hold parts of cities in the west, including neighbourhoods of the former commercial hub Aleppo, and Kurdish groups control some northern regions.\par \par \uc2 "In every street in Damascus there is a different rate," the Damascus businessman said. "In Homs, Aleppo, Damascus, the black market rate varies."\par \par \uc2 He held out little hope of recovery for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without an end to hostilities. "Can you say whether the Geneva peace talks will succeed, or whether the armed groups will stop fighting?" he said. (Writing by Dominic Evans; editing by Andrew Roche) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-3-18T16:07:16.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160318ec3i01ep3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
