\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Russia's ex-Soviet neighbours struggle to contain rouble, commodity fallout\b0\par\par\uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2016\par 12:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 27 (Reuters) - Georgian authorities sold dollars this week for the third time in 2016 and promised more monetary policy tightening to lift the lari off record lows to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Over the past two years, falling commodity prices and the precipitous decline in the value of the rouble have forced most of Russia's former Soviet trading partners into devaluations to keep their economies competitive and prevent reserves from being drained dry.\par \par \uc2 Most rely heavily on Russia for trade, investment and remittances. The rouble fell 20 percent last year versus the dollar after shedding 43 percent in 2014. This year it is already down 7 percent.\par \par \uc2 Here is a list of moves by central banks across the former Soviet Union this year:\par \par \uc2 GEORGIA - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gyorgy Kadagidze said on Jan 26 the lari would stabilise in the near future. The bank also sold $20 million, its third direct \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, after the lari hit a new record low of 2.4984 per dollar, having fallen from 1.75 in November 2014. Kadagidze also pledged more monetary tightening if needed. The lari fell more than 20 percent last year when authorities sold $300 million and raised interest rates several times to 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 AZERBAIJAN - Plans to issue $500 million in bonds to cover a budget deficit caused by oil price falls. It has regularly sold dollars to lift the manat, last selling $39 million on Jan 25. Earlier it banned \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales in standalone bureaux de change and said it would impose limits on hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  outflows and a 20 percent tax on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exports related to overseas investment, real estate and securities purchases. The manat was devalued by 32 percent on December 21.\par \par \uc2 KAZAKHSTAN - The tenge has fallen to record lows beyond 380 per dollar, having weakened steadily after a devaluation of more than a third last August. It has already lost 10 percent this year following a 46 percent loss in 2015. The country's sovereign wealth fund said oil prices at $20 a barrel would send the tenge rate to 420 UKRAINE - The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Jan 25 it would sell $30 million to stabilise the hryvnia which fell to 10-month lows to the dollar. It sold $40 million and almost $100 million on Jan. 19 and Jan 15 respectively. The hryvnia is down more than 65 percent since early-2014. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  abandoned dollar sales last February to preserve scarce reserves, jacked up interest rates and tightened \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. MOLDOVA - Moldova's leu is near record lows to the dollar after slumping 20 percent in 2015. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates six times last year while its governor resigned in August over the disappearance of $1 billion from the country's banking system. TURKMENISTAN - Exchange bureaux in Turkmenistan stopped selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Jan. 12, citing a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  order, prompting a jump in black market rates as fears grew of a devaluation. Turkmenistan, whose main export is natural gas, devalued the manat by about 19 percent to 3.50 manats per dollar from Jan. 1, 2015. BELARUS - The Belarus rouble has fallen to record lows to the dollar, losing than 30 percent last year. The country, which has extremely close trade links to \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said last month that it would keep \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a minimum in 2016 to avoid "excessive strengthening" of the rouble. UZBEKISTAN - The sum weakened around 10 percent last year to the dollar and has lost another 2 percent since the start of 2016, according to Reuters data. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  arrangements are non-transparent. The \uc2 IMF\uc2  describes the sum's exchange rate regime as a "crawl-like arrangement".\par \par \uc2 TAJIKISTAN - The somoni closely tracks the rouble and has fallen almost 15 percent already so far in 2016 after losing 20 percent in 2015 as the flow of remittances from Russia shrank. Last month, authorities shut down all \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange offices, stipulating that \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions mnust be carried out at official branches of banks. * Most of these countries are members or associate states of the \uc2 Commonwealth of Independent States\uc2 , a grouping of former Soviet Union republics. Georgia has left the group.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Sujata Rao and Marc Jones) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-27T12:12:58.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160127ec1r00g4q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
