\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID EUROPE-Pension test for Greece\b0\par\par\uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 2016\par 09:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * A look at the day ahead from European Economics and Politics Editor Mark John and EMEA Markets Editor Mike Dolan. The views expressed are their own.\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Jan 26 (Reuters) - Greece launches a parliament debate on a reform of its heavily indebted pension system today, the opening salvo of what may be the toughest test yet of \uc2 Alexis Tsipras\uc2 's staying power as prime minister. Pension reform is key to the first review of Greece's third bailout, in turn leading to long-awaited talks on debt relief. But the Greeks have already had their pensions cut about a dozen times - about 30 percent on average since the country's financial crisis erupted in 2010. Farmers are already on the streets threatening mass blockades and nationwide walkouts while opposition parties are vowing to block the reforms.\par \par \uc2 Danish parties will vote later on an immigration bill which among things gives authorities the right to delay family reunification and confiscate refugees' possessions in downpayment for their stay. The text has been watered down to exempt jewellery of sentimental value but has been attacked by the United Nations and human rights groups. The measures are likely to pass with an overwhelming majority, a sign of a wider hardening of Nordic attitudes on the refugee issue.\par \par \uc2 The annual news conference of Russia's wily foreign minister Sergey Lavrov, who in his 11 years in the job has become a past master at wrongfooting and infuriating the West on everything from the Middle East to missile defence, should be interesting. He is likely to field questions on everything from \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Syria to the situation in eastern Ukraine and no doubt the most recent tensions with Britain over an investigation that concluded the killing of ex-KGB agent Alexander Litvinenko on British soil was probably directed by Moscow.\par \par \uc2 MARKETS AT 0745 GMT\par \par \uc2 The latest attempt at stabilization fades again as oil retreats back below $30 and Shanghai shares plunge 6.4%. In fairness, any oil rally based on snow was never going to be durable and the supply glut was amply reinforced by Iraqi production estimates. The demand side of the story was hit by news of big drops in China rail freight volumes and coal imports earlier, concerns about which were best captured by Germany's Ifo business survey for January yesterday that showed not only a dent to confidence from China growth concerns but also from the global stock market ructions this month. U.S. January consumer confidence and PMIs out later will be keenly watched for a similar impact. Below-forecast Q4 South Korea GDP numbers earlier didn't help the mood either, while the Chinese markets themselves may well be unsettled by the approach of the Lunar New Year holidays there and attempts by the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to ease liquidity concerns. With Brent crude trading as low as $29.35, the latest commodities lunge and the Shanghai shakeout swept across Asia bourses. Tokyo and Hong Kong indices were off more than 2 pct, while Seoul's Kospi fell more than 1 pct. The South Korean won underperformed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Wall St had already turned sour overnight, with the S&P500 ending down 1.56 pct into a heavy earnings week that sees Apple report after the bell later. And with S&P futures down another 0.5 pct at this early stage, eurostocks are expected to open almost 2 pct lower. In emerging markets, Russia's rouble and the Kazakh tenge are on the slide again. \uc2 Nigeria's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting later will be closely eyed as pressure to devalue the official naira rate in line with the plummeting black-market rate have intensified. No change is expected at \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting meantime.\par \par \uc2 Upcoming events/data/ themes for market reports on Tuesday:\par \par \uc2 - European corp events: Dixons, \uc2 easyJet\uc2 , \uc2 Siemens\uc2 , Philips\par \par \uc2 - Swiss Dec trade data\par \par \uc2 - Sweden Dec PPI\par \par \uc2 - BoE's Carney testifies\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Irish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  updates growth forecasts, new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Lane speaks\par \par \uc2 - Hungary rate decision\par \par \uc2 - Poland Dec jobless\par \par \uc2 - Nigeria rate decision\par \par \uc2 - Turkey's quarterly inflation report, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  minutes\par \par \uc2 - Brazil Dec current account\par \par \uc2 - US Q4 earnings: Apple, Corning, \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2 , J&J, Proctor & Gamble, \uc2 Janus Capital\uc2  etc\par \par \uc2 - US Jan consumer confidence, flash PMIs, Nov house prices\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  auctions 2-yr notes (Editing by Sonya Hepinstall)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-29T08:10:05.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160126ec1q00796\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
