\par\par \b \uc2 Russian Officials Seek to Reassure Markets They Have Control Over Falling Ruble\b0\par\par\uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2016\par 20:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW\u8212\'20\'14Top Russian officials Friday sought to reassure markets they have control over the ruble and that its drop to all-time lows has a silver lining.\par \par \uc2 The Russian ruble hit record lows against the U.S. dollar this week amid a drop in prices for oil\u8212\'20\'14the country's key export\u8212\'20\'14and poor economic fundamentals. But officials have been quick to declare that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's collapse, hitting ordinary Russians squarely in the pocketbook, isn't a catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Prime Minister Yury Trutnev put a positive spin on Russia's rapidly depreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, saying that the plunging ruble would open potential opportunities for investors.\par \par \uc2 "Devaluation is not very good for the population of Russia, but is very good for investors as their costs are falling," Mr. Trutnev said at the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  in Davos, Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trutnev, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's emissary to the country's Far Eastern Federal Region, also didn't offer specifics on how the government would respond to the crisis, but said reforms should be aimed at "creating a competitive environment for business."\par \par \uc2 On Friday, thanks to a rebound in Brent crude futures above $30 per barrel, the ruble rallied nearly 6% to 78 versus the dollar, a level last seen on Monday. The ruble had weakened to as low as 86 against the dollar on Thursday, losing 60% of its value since Moscow's annexation of Crimea in March 2014.\par \par \uc2 But after the ruble crossed the crucial psychological threshold of 80 to the dollar, Bank of Russia Gov. Elvira Nabiullina canceled a planned visit to Davos this week.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a meeting with government officials at the finance ministry on Friday, Ms. Nabiullina said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will try to douse any excessive volatility in the ruble while it has the tools to mitigate any risks to the country's financial stability.\par \par \uc2 "We have full set of tools to act proactively and avoid threats to financial stability," Ms. Nabiullina said, without elaborating.\par \par \uc2 This latest verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows earlier comments from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the depreciation was justified and carried no risks to financial stability. The statement was taken by the market as a direct indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no immediate plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with selling dollars to stem the decline in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Despite a continuing drop in real disposable incomes amid double-digit inflation, the finance ministry is preparing to involve ordinary Russians in the budget-planning process. Konstantin Vyshkovsky, the head of the ministry's state debt department, said Friday that the finance ministry is planning to issue domestic bonds to plug holes in its oil-dependent budget.\par \par \uc2 Given ordinary Russians' "low tolerance" for risks, the new bonds won't be traded on the market, Mr. Vyshkovsky said. He didn't say when and in what amount the finance ministry plans to issue them.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Anton Siluanov\uc2  said Russia will have to increase its borrowing, but said that the budget deficit shouldn't exceed 3% in the long term. For this year, despite a drop in oil prices below $30 per barrel, Mr. Putin also set a budget deficit ceiling at 3%.\par \par \uc2 Former Finance Minister Alexei Kudrin, a confidant of Mr. Putin, said at the Davos forum on Friday that the peak of the current crisis is still ahead. The crisis was "a very painful thing for ordinary Russians" who have seen their earning power evaporate.\par \par \uc2 "I am very concerned that the real income of the population will fall, the living standards," he said.\par \par \uc2 The crisis, Mr. Kudrin suggested, could potentially alter the Kremlin's assertive foreign policy. Mr. Putin last year seized global attention with his military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Syria, and is viewed as an essential player in resolving crises in the Middle East and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 "We now economically have become weaker," Mr. Kudrin said. "And subsequently, it may affect both Russia's defense capacity and its role in the foreign policy."\par \par \uc2 Write to Nathan Hodge at nathan.hodge@wsj.com and Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 22, 2016 14:15 ET (19:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160122ec1m003tp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
