\par\par \b \uc2 Russia's Syria Move Could Hit Oil Hard -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2015\par 23:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 17:40 EDT - What will knock crude oil out of the $44-$47/bbl range it's been stuck in since Sept. 1? Gabriel Collins, an attorney at \uc2 Baker and Hostetler\uc2  in Houston, says it could be \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Syria, and says prices could tumble toward $30/bbl by the new year. "Amid worries that \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's armed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Syria signals a new Cold War between \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the West, another and related rematch appears in the offing," he says on OilPro. "If Russia and Iran are Syrian President Assad's strongest foreign backers, Saudi Arabia and its Gulf Arab allies surely number among his and Iran's most implacable foes. The implications of a potential Russo-Saudi proxy war for international oil markets are significant." (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2138 GMT - The Australian dollar may work its way back towards near-term resistance should the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to endorse a wait-and-see approach for monetary policy at its policy meeting, says David Song, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at DailyFX. A survey of 11 economist by WSJ shows all expect no change in the cash rate target, now at 2%. Despite below-trend growth in the economy, the RBA may continue to endorse a wait-and-see approach as the depreciation in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to support growth, particularly through a larger contribution from net service exports, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may stick to the sidelines throughout 2015, he adds. Policy decision due at 0330 GMT. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 17:36 EDT - \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  (CAT) is among the US exporters most bullish about the new Trans-Pacific Partnership deal. If the deal is ratified, CAT says, Vietnam's 15% import tariff on bulldozers would be eliminated, for example, saving buyers of \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2 's D-8 models $150,000. In Malaysia, tariffs of 30% on engines and 15% on spare parts would vanish. CAT says the trade deal generally would spur trade. CAT closed up 5.3% to $69.17. (bob.hagerty@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:23 EDT - It's one of the most persistent debates in the oil market: Is gasoline consumption driven by oil prices, or by economic growth? Clearview Energy Partners crunches some numbers and tentatively comes down on the side of price. "In the ten states responsible for roughly 60% of year-on-year US gasoline consumption growth, consumption gains appear more closely linked to price decreases than to structural factors such as declining unemployment rates and rising personal incomes," the firm says. "As such, a rebound in global crude prices may have potential to reverse these consumption gains." (nicole.friedman@wsj.com; @NicoleFriedman)\par \par \uc2 17:22 EDT - Investors are underestimating the potential boost to Donaco's (DNA.AU) earnings from the casino operator's deal with China's Heng Sheng Group at the Star Vegas casino in Cambodia, says Canaccord Genuity. The deal, which likely means more Chinese nationals traveling to Donaco's casino, boosts the number of gambling tables at the venue by 28% to 137. "While our current estimates for annualized ebitda of A$10 million reflect 'base case' assumptions, our 'bull case' indicates the Heng Sheng VIP room could be more than 20% EPS accretive in FY17," analyst Aaron Muller says. The broker keeps a buy call on Donaco and A$1.54/share price target. DNA last at A$0.69. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 17:20 EDT - \uc2 BP\uc2  agrees to pay a record $20B to resolve all federal and state claims against the company over its role in the Deepwater Horizon oil spill. Under the terms of the finalized deal announced by the Justice Department today, \uc2 BP\uc2  won't have to pay all the money at once. Softening the hit to its cash flow, the company is able to spread out payments over a 15-year period. The last installments are due in 2031, more than 21 years after the Deepwater Horizon disaster, which killed 11 crew members and caused the largest oil spill in US waters. The two biggest pieces of the settlement are the $5.5B in Clean Water Act penalties and the $7.1B that \uc2 BP\uc2  agreed to pay to the US and Gulf Coast states to cover long-term environmental damages. The total settlement figure also includes $1B that \uc2 BP\uc2  previously committed for early restoration projects. (jacob.gershman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:19 EDT - Ruling in favor of a former \uc2 UBS\uc2  employee, Connecticut's highest court says today that public and private employees in the state are shielded by speech rights more expansive than the First Amendment. The Connecticut Supreme Court rules that government and private-sector employers are barred from punishing workers for statements they make as part of their jobs on matters of important public concern. At issue was a complaint brought against \uc2 UBS\uc2  by a former employee who questioned the valuation of properties held in \uc2 UBS\uc2 's real-estate investment arm. A UBS spokeswoman said the company "believes that Mr. Trusz's claims are wholly without merit, and the firm did not improperly act against him." She said \uc2 UBS\uc2  would continue to "vigorously" defend itself in the matter. Wesley W. Horton, a lawyer for Trusz, says today's decision is the first by a state high court that gives "employees a free-speech right to speak out in an area that involves their official duties." (jacob.gershman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2114 GMT - Weak inflation will likely drive \uc2 New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut its policy rate by another 25 bps to 2.5% by year-end, says Christina Leung, senior economist at the New Zealand Institute of Economic Research. She says a rate cut in October, however, "is a line-ball" call, and the RBNZ might opt to hold off until December. She says an official cash rate at 2.5% is an "emergency setting" and she doesn't expect rates to go any lower. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2108 GMT - New Zealand business confidence deteriorates in 3Q, but the drop is in stark contrast to firms' own trading activity, according to the New Zealand Institute of Economic Research's Quarterly Survey of Business Opinion. A seasonally adjusted net 9% of firms now expect business conditions to deteriorate over the next six months versus a net 6% that had expected an improvement in 2Q, NZIER says. However, a seasonally adjusted net 12% of businesses reported an improvement in their own trading activity over the past quarter. Even more positive, was the rebound in businesses' expected trading activity over the past quarter, with a net 17% expecting an improvement. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 17:03 EDT - Shares of genetic analysis company Illumina (ILMN) fall after the company guides 3Q and 4Q revenue below the Wall Street consensus. It blames weaker-than-expected sales of its desktop gene-sequencers adding that it was "disappointed with instrument sales in Europe and continued weakness in the Asia-Pacific region." The warning follows a 2Q sales miss in July which surprised the Street because of the company's habit of consistently beating expectations. ILMN shares are off 14% at $140 after hours, down $100 since the day before it missed Wall Street's 2Q sales predictions (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com; @patmsheridan)\par \par \uc2 16:59 EDT - The rainfall in the Carolinas has brought the Eastern railroads more trouble. \uc2 CSX\uc2  tells its customers this afternoon that two of its major lines and several branch lines had been affected by flooding and fallen trees. While crews worked to fix the problems and restore \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the railroad says, "the risk of additional rain and flooding continues." The railroad adds that freight has been slowed but is still moving in most areas, though some local customers in the region may not get service until the tracks are back to normal. (betsy.morris@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:53 EDT - New videogame hardware will soon be taking up shelf space alongside \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's Xbox and \uc2 Sony\uc2 's PlayStation machines at \uc2 GameStop\uc2  (GME) stores. Closely held \uc2 Valve Corp\uc2 . today announced that starting Nov. 10 \uc2 GameStop\uc2  will be the exclusive nondigital U.S. retailer of its new Steam Machines -- consoles that play games sold through Valve's online store called Steam, which is popular with PC gamers. For the past three years, \uc2 GameStop\uc2  has been selling prepaid Steam cards with codes for downloading games. A Valve spokesman says sales of the cards -- available for $20, $50 or $100 -- have more than doubled each year, declining to share details. (sarah.needleman@wsj.com; @saraheneedleman)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 05, 2015 17:40 ET (21:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151005eba5003hs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
