\par\par \b \uc2 European Midday Briefing: Stocks Rebound Gathers Momentum\b0\par\par\uc2 1,548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2015\par 12:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par European Markets: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stock markets rallied on the first day of the new quarter, extending their rebound from heavy declines into a second session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Stoxx Europe 600 was up 1.0% midmorning. In the eurozone, manufacturing data showed the pace of expansion slowed slightly in September.\par \par \uc2 "Much of the uncertainty is already in the price. Investors are well aware of the impending economic slowdown," said \uc2 Jeremy Batstone-Carr\uc2  at \uc2 Charles Stanley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mining stocks, which have taken a battering in recent weeks, led the way in Europe. The Stoxx Europe 600 basic resources subindex, which is full of companies that highly sensitive to Chinese demand, climbed 2.7%.\par \par \uc2 Shares in commodities trader and producer \uc2 Glencore\uc2  continued to recover following a plunge earlier in the week, rising 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, investors were looking ahead to Friday's U.S. jobs report, which is closely watched by \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials as they decide when to raise short-term interest rates. \uc2 The Fed\uc2 's decision to hold rates at record lows in September helped fuel fears of slowing global growth.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect 200,000 jobs were created in September and estimate that the unemployment rate held at 5.1%.\par \par \uc2 Investors are also awaiting the coming third-quarter earnings season. A disappointing batch of corporate profit news could cause the recent rebound in markets to fade quickly, said Mr. Batstone-Carr.\par \par \uc2 "I'd be concerned about sounding the all clear, particularly as we head into the reporting season," he said.\par \par \uc2 In commodity news, oil prices rose in tandem with other commodities after sentiment was boosted by a modest improvement in economic data out of China.\par \par \uc2 Markets were also tracking the developments in Syria, where \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched its first airstrikes on Wednesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  added to the uncertainty in the Middle East, the world's biggest oil producing region.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Syrian conflict has increased the geopolitical risks, which is giving tailwind to the prices," analysts at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  said in a research note to clients.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude, the global oil benchmark, rose 1.4% to $49.73 a barrel on London's ICE Futures exchange. On the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2 , West Texas Intermediate futures were trading up 1.9% at $45.96 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices were trading down 0.1% at $1,113.65/oz, after stronger-than-expected U.S. employment data supported the likelihood of an interest rate rise before the year-end.\par \par \uc2 "[Thursday] sees Initial Jobless Claims and U.S. Manufacturing PMI, along with ISM Manufacturing, so [there is] scope for a few moves in the dollar and hence the precious metals," said David Govett, head of precious metals trading at Marex Spectron, in a note. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par American Markets Outlook: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks were poised for another day with solid gains, with futures tracking Asian and European shares higher after Chinese manufacturing data beat forecasts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Futures for the Dow jumped 132 points, or 0.8%, while those for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 15.30 points, or 0.8%. Futures for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2  climbed 47.50 points, or 1.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The indicated increases would mark a third straight day of gains for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Dow, and a second day of gains for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2 , after all three benchmarks closed out Wednesday firmly in positive territory. However, the advances that session weren't enough to swing the averages higher for the quarter, and they ended with the sharpest quarterly losses in four years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thursday's early upbeat trading mood followed a better-than-expected reading on Chinese factory activity, although analysts cautioned that the country's economy is still likely to have slowed substantially in 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Economic growth in emerging economies has weakened substantially this year, and China may well see its growth slow to 6.8% this year from 7.3%, and the PMI data tends to confirm this," said Brenda Kelly at \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Capital Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was stronger against the euro, which declined to $1.1156 from $1.1177 late Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was tracking weakness that followed the eurozone's consumer prices in September, which fell year-over-year for the first time since the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  launched its massive asset-purchase program in March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The WSJ Dollar Index, a measure of the dollar against a basket of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was up 0.06% at 88.82.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar rose broadly between July and September as U.S. economic data generally outpaced those of rival economies and as commodity prices continued to spiral lower, pulling down the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of countries that export raw materials.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the buck slipped against the euro and the yen over the period, as rising uncertainty over global growth and the Fed's timeline for raising rates led investors to pull back on their largest dollar bets. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =========================== \par Top Stories: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moscow Maintains Airstrikes Targeted \f28 \uc2 \f2 Islamic State\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian officials dismissed Western assertions that Moscow wasn't targeting \f28 \uc2 \f2 Islamic State\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Syria but other opponents of Bashar al-Assad's regime, and said they had no information on possible civilian casualties.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2  Shares Regain More Lost Ground\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Glencore PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  shares have recouped the ground they lost in heavy selling earlier this week, vindicating management's efforts to restore confidence in the commodities group's finances amid weak commodities prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 VW Officials Meet Over Probe as Crisis Hits \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Key directors at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Volkswagen\f28 \uc2 \f2  met late into Wednesday night to review initial findings of an internal investigation into emissions-rigging at the car maker, as effects of the scandal began hitting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ============================ \par Analysis: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Europe, Car Makers Have Close Ties with Testers\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The scramble to tighten rules on emissions tests following \f28 \uc2 \f2 Volkswagen\f28 \uc2 \f2 's diesel-engine scandal is laying bare a regime in which car makers pay the very firms that test and certify their vehicles.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coal is Problem for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Other Miners\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With prices at an eight-year low and China's purchases declining, the pressure is on miners that took on debt to snap up assets when coal prices were higher. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ============================ \par Still To Come \par GMT                    Exp   Prev \par 1230 US      Weekly Export Sales \par              Corn          854.9K \par              Soybeans     1325.8K \par              Wheat         529.6K \par 1230 US      Weekly Jobless \par              Claims     271K 267K \par              Net Chg          +3K \par              Continuing \par              Claims       2242000 \par              Net Chg          -1K \par 1330 CAN Sep Manufacturing PMI \par 1330 US      G24 Committee of \par              the Whole meeting \par 1345 US  Sep Manufacturing \par              PMI          52.9 53 \par 1345 US      Bloomberg Consumer \par              Comfort Index \par 1400 US      \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  President \par              \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jim Yong Kim\f28 \uc2 \f2  speech \par 1400 US Aug  New Construction \par                        +0.6% 0.7% \par 1400 US Sep  ISM Manufacturing \par              Report on Business \par              Manufacturing \par                         50.8 51.1 \par              Prices            39 \par              Employment      51.2 \par              Inventories     48.5 \par              New Orders      51.7 \par              Production      53.6 \par 1430 US      EIA Weekly Natural \par              Gas Storage Report \par              Working Gas    3440B \par              Net Chg        +106B \par 1500 US  Sep Global \par              Manufacturing   50.7 \par 2000 US  Sep Domestic Auto \par              Industry \par              Sales   17.6M 17.81M \par 2030 US      Money Stock Measures \par 2030 US      Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central \par              Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Holdings \par 2030 US      Federal Discount \par              Window Borrowings \par 2330 JPN Aug Jobless \par              Rate       3.3% 3.3% \par 2350 JPN Sep Monetary Base \par N/A  US      San Francisco Fed \par              President John \par              Williams speech \par ============================ \par  \par Global News: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Factory Outlook Still Gloomy Despite Uptick\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Persistent headwinds are holding back the world's second-largest economy despite some nascent signs of improvement in September for China's struggling manufacturing sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ's Tankan Shows Greater Caution at Big Manufacturers\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's latest corporate survey shows weakening optimism among big manufacturers as concerns mount over China's sharpening economic slowdown. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Other News: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bayer to Raise Less Than Forecast From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Covestro\f28 \uc2 \f2  Listing\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German pharmaceuticals and chemicals giant Bayer said it would raise $1.12 billion less than previously forecast from the stock market listing of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Covestro\f28 \uc2 \f2 , its specialty chemicals business, because of market volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Regulators Query Ball Corp.'s Proposed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rexam\f28 \uc2 \f2  Takeover\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  regulators are concerned that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ball Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s planned $4.9 billion acquisition of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rexam PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  could lead to higher prices for beverage cans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary Urges Other Regions to Share Migrant Burden\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary's leader proposed that migrants should be relocated not only within Europe but also to countries outside the continent whose policies have contributed to the migration crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recalling Ukraine, Latvian Leader Warns on Putin\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Latvia's president warned the West against trusting Moscow as a partner as Russia expands its role in Syria.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  to Pay $2.5 Million Penalty Over Swaps Reporting\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The CFTC said it ordered \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  to pay a $2.5 million civil penalty to settle claims the bank failed to properly report swaps transactions between January 2013 and July of this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Afghan Forces Largely Recapture Kunduz From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taliban\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Provincial police spokesman Sayed Sarwar Hussaini said the center of Kunduz had been completely cleared of militants but that fighting was ongoing to remove remaining fighters from its outskirts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Abbas Says Palestinians No Longer Are Bound by Oslo Accord\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Palestinian Authority President Mahmoud Abbas said Palestinians are no longer bound by the 1995 Oslo II Accord, which established the foundation of a two-state solution, because of Israel's failure to implement it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Sarka Halas at sarka.halas@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 01, 2015 06:40 ET (10:40 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151001eba10012y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
