\par\par \b \uc2 Russia's Economy Shows no Sign of Recovery\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2015\par 18:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW\u8212\'20\'14Russia's troubled economy showed no sign of recovery in July as falling oil prices continued to hurt the commodity-dependent country, data showed on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Two key economic drivers, retail sales and capital investment, continued to decline in July, putting more pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates to revive the tanking economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces a difficult decision at its meeting next month over whether to hold rates to support the ruble.\par \par \uc2 Battered by the fall in the price of oil, Russia's key export, the ruble lost more than 13% of its value against the dollar over the past month, sliding to its weakest level since February. The Kremlin and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in with a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , claiming that authorities were doing all they could to cap the free fall, but that provided little support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tool kit to prop up the ruble is limited. Kremlin aide \uc2 Andrei Belousov\uc2  said this week that the only instrument it still possesses to steer the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is its key rate. Mr. Belousov added that Moscow does not have sufficient reserves to artificially support the ruble. Reserves have fallen to $360 billion from nearly $540 billion two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Tom Levinson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Sberbank CIB\uc2  in Moscow, said that after slashing rates five times since early 2015, the Bank of Russia may pause at the September 11 meeting. It may want to see if the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raises rates later in September, he said.\par \par \uc2 Other analysts say that a pause in the monetary easing-cycle is likely because the economy needs time to feel the impact of earlier rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 "Economic growth indicators signal extended recession," \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 So far, the economic pattern in Russia has changed little compared with the second quarter. Retail sales in Russia, which rely on the main growth driver of consumer demand, fell 9.2% on the year in July after growing by 1.6% in the same month a year ago. In the first seven months of 2015, retail sales declined by 8.1%, data from the Federal Statistics Service showed on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Data also showed that gross domestic product shrank by 3.4% in the first half of 2015. Earlier this month, data showed that economic contraction deepened to 4.6% in the second quarter from 2.2% in the first three months of the year.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday that Russia's economic prospects look worse than they used to because potential output is limited by structural issues in the economy, which can not be addressed by monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Underpinning the view that the recession will be prolonged, capital investment plunged 8.5% on the year in July after falling by 7.1% in the preceding month.\par \par \uc2 While the government still expects the economy to contract by as much as 3% this year, the Bank of Russia said last week that it will present a new set of economic forecasts on September 11. The revision is likely to paint a bleaker economic outlook, given that Brent crude prices are trading around $48 per barrel from levels of around $60 seen earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 The data also showed that real wages, or salaries adjusted for inflation, fell 8.2% in July compared with the same month a year ago after falling by 7.2% in June. Real disposable incomes declined by 2% in July.\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 19, 2015 12:55 ET (16:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150819eb8j002dz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
