\par\par \b \uc2 Moscow Won't Let Ruble Firm Before Economy Recovers -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 181 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 2015\par 14:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1253 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Though the Bank of Russia claims it does not target a specific ruble rate, many analysts share the view that Moscow is interested in keeping the ruble weak as it helps to limit budget shortfall from commodity exports. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't let the ruble strengthen before economic activity recovers, even if oil prices climb higher. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  sees USD/RUB rising to 60 this year and to 63 next year from levels around 55.4 Monday due to accommodative fiscal and monetary policies together with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Expects \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's GDP to contract by 3.6% in 2015 and then grow by 0.4% in 2016. This is more pessimistic than the government call for a swift economic recovery next year after a contraction of up to 3% this year.(andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 29, 2015 08:53 ET (12:53 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150629eb6t001ip\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
