\par\par \b \uc2 Will May Durable Goods Show Some Pickup? -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,548 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2015\par 13:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7:30 EDT - The April durable-goods report showed a sign of optimism regarding business investment. Will today's print on May include some follow-through? If so, it would be a further sign the 1Q softness was restricted to then. But there's been some readings which have raised questions about whether there's been a noted pickup this quarter for the US economy. That uncertainty has continued to push out some analysts' forecasts on when the rate-hike cycle begins. \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  futures are up 5 while oil and gold are little changed despite a stronger dollar; the euro has slid to session lows just above $1.12-even. Treasurys have weakened a bit more, with the 10-year yield at 2.38%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 1118 GMT Demand for bond funds jumped to EUR77 billion, up from EUR20 billion in the previous quarter, according to Europe's funds association Efama. "Net sales of multi-asset funds also posted a strong rise in net inflows during the quarter to EUR101 billion, up from EUR19 billion in the fourth quarter," the association says in a release. Meanwhile, equity funds registered a turnaround in net sales to post EUR39 billion of inflows, compared to EUR5 billion of outflows in the previous quarter. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1105 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand is expected to soon see an end of the current bout of deflation, along with other Asian economies that have shared similar experience in the past several months, as a result of lower energy prices. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daniel Martin\f28 \uc2 \f2  from Capital Economics says in a research note that negative inflation in Thailand should be viewed as "good" deflation, which is caused by "an external or supply-side shock and can help boost demand in an economy by putting more money in the pockets of consumers and raising corporate profits", just as has happened to Singapore, Taiwan and South Korea. "As the impact of downward pressure from lower energy prices starts to fade towards the end of the year, we expect all of these economies to exit deflation," says Martin. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:00 EDT - Brazilians now substantially favor elected officials not being able to be re-elected, a poll shows. Datafolha says 67% of Brazilians are in favor of single terms, versus 33% a decade ago and 39% in 2007, the last time the polling institute asked about the issue. The surge comes as approval ratings for President Rousseff, re-elected last fall, have hit record lows amid the country's weak economy and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Petrobras\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PBR) corruption scandal. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6:46 EDT - Following sizable trade deficits to start 2015, Brazil is now in surplus for the year. But it's not for a good reason. Through Sunday, the country has exported $914M more of goods than it imported, says the trade ministry. That as imports have slumped 18% amid Brazil's moribund economy. Exports have fallen 15% from a year earlier. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1042 GMT The latest economic crisis in Russia has led to a change in behavior that will have a lasting impact well beyond previous crises, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 PricewaterhouseCoopers\f28 \uc2 \f2 . According to a survey it conducted, 90% of Russians have noticed a decrease in purchasing power and 53% can no longer save. Since the crisis began, 32% of Russians buy less food, 31% have switched to cheaper brands, 24% buy more discounted produce and 19% of Russians buy food products at stores with lower prices, it says. Ordinary Russians believe that the crisis will last around two years while companies see some improvement in the next six months. (Olga.Razumovskaya@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1035 GMT The good news about Greece is that both sides see the proposals presented by Athens on Monday morning as a basis for a potential deal, says Holger Schmieding, chief economist at Berenberg. The bad news is that Greece is putting forward the wrong kind of deal, he says. Within the overall parameters set by creditors, Athens proposes almost exclusively tax rises rather than pro-growth structural reforms and well-targeted expenditure cuts. "This would repeat the key mistake of the early 2010-2013 bailout programs for Greece, namely to hit aggregate demand too hard instead of raising supply fast," Mr. Schmieding says. Nonetheless, whether or not there will be a deal in the next few days remains unclear, the economist adds.(emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1030 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Thailand's Transport Minister Prajin Juntong is not sure if the country will be able to resolve the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Civil Aviation Organization\f28 \uc2 \f2 's air safety concerns about Thailand after its audit in January, as earlier hoped. "The improvement of personnel is a time-consuming process," says Prajin. The warning was made public on its safety audit website last week after Thailand was unable to provide sufficient safety oversight, particularly regarding air operator certification (AOC) procedures, within 90 days. Prajin adds that Thailand now awaits overseas experts with ICAO-recommended licenses to arrive and help train local staffers and inspect the AOC issuance process--all steps that will need some time to complete. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1027 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  says Singapore's core inflation measure will likely "hit an inflection point" in the third quarter as the oil price recovery pushes utility tariffs higher, while food prices may rise if the El Nino weather phenomenon strikes production. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore\f28 \uc2 \f2 's core inflation measure, which strips out accommodation and private road transport costs, eased to 0.1% in May from 0.4% in April and 1.0% in March. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the decline in the pace of core inflation is mainly due to administrative measures such as a cut in the maid levy and lower food prices in May. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  says it continues to expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "to keep policy on hold in October, and would only expect a change in the event of a significant darkening in the growth outlook." (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1021 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Continued weakening of Thailand's industrial confidence only highlights a grimmer outlook for the country's sluggish economy during the remainder of the year. The Thai Industries Sentiment Index fell for the fifth consecutive month in May -- to 85.4, the lowest level in a year -- due to worries over slow global economic recovery and the impact of drought on local purchasing power. "It seems that the economic recovery is now skewed to the downside more than previously anticipated, especially private domestic demand and exports of goods," says Sarun Sunansathaporn, an economist at Tisco Economic Strategic Unit, in an email. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  last week revised downward its 2015 GDP growth estimate to 3.0% from 3.8% and projected this year's exports to contract 1.5% -- down from the earlier forecast of 0.8% growth. (nopparat.chaichalearmmongkol@wsj.com; Twitter: @nopbkk) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: WWE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1018 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Things aren't looking up for export-dependent Taiwan. Export orders fell 5.9% in May and orders from mainland China and Hong Kong, which combined make up Taiwan's biggest market, continued to slip, falling by 11.6% on year in May versus 10.3% in April. The results are "disappointing," says ANZ, which forecasted exports would rise by 0.1%. Demand for Taiwanese electronic components -- once a mainstay in the majority of laptops and cellphones around the world -- is "losing steam," says ANZ. Weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Japan and Korea may also have dragged demand for Taiwan's exports, as the three countries export similar items. The volcanic island nation has struggled in recent years with anemic growth. "External demand has not yet bottomed out as previously anticipated and momentum remains weak," said ANZ. (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: DAH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1009 GMT The Bank of Russia is paying attention to future U.S. Federal Reserve rate decisions in its report on financial stability, saying a rate increase may prompt Moscow to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. But that would be a last resort measure as the widely expected monetary tightening in the U.S. will have only limited impact on emerging markets, says Ksenia Yudayeva, first deputy chairwoman at the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Russia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . When asked about possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the Fed move, Ms. Yudayeva says all is possible but the ruble's crisis of late 2014, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  crashed to its record lows, is unlikely to repeat again. In December, the dollar rose above 80 ruble for the first time, soaring from around 33 in early 2014. Tuesday, the dollar is up 0.7% at 54.31 rubles. (andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 23, 2015 07:30 ET (11:30 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150623eb6n00163\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
