\par\par \b \uc2 Russia Weathers Financial Risks but More Remain, Bank of Russia Says\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrey Ostroukh   \par \uc2 947 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2015\par 11:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MOSCOW--Russia's sufficient reserves and flexible monetary policy ensures the country's financial stability for now, but a possible interest-rate increase in the U.S. and unpredictable oil prices keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Russia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Facing soaring inflation and a contracting economy, the Bank of Russia has been increasingly active in adjusting its monetary policy over the past months in an attempt to preserve financial stability. The latest report reckons that Russia's financial system is shielded from existing risks but its future looks uncertain.\par \par \uc2 In a semiannual report on financial stability, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  noted Tuesday that, in the fourth quarter of 2014 and the first three months of 2015, Russia endured a number of risks to stability. They ranged from the more expected causes, such as Russia's rating downgrade and a lack of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to repayment of foreign debt, to such unexpected reasons as a rapid drop in global oil prices.\par \par \uc2 "The financial system turned to be resilient to external shocks, which is related to better banking regulation and financial market developments of the past few years," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 As an average oil price of $60 to $65 per barrel is comfortable enough for Russian oil companies and the budget, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said, it is now preparing to address external shocks related to debt issues in Europe as well as risks linked to monetary policy of the U.S. Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Russia, which cites a Wall Street Journal poll of economists on Fed policy, said a possible increase in the U.S. interest rate in September will make it more expensive for emerging economies to service dollar-denominated debt. Higher rates in the U.S. will put selling pressure on such emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the ruble, and may spur capital outflows, the bank said.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Russia said it can't rule out another wave of volatility on global markets on the back of higher U.S. rates, which could prompt the \uc2 Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and to boost lending of dollars domestically.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, emerging market economies are likely to continue dovish monetary policies given risks stemming from poor domestic demand. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Russia is also widely expected to ease its policy further, after four rate cuts brought the key rate to 11.5% in early June.\par \par \uc2 Though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's and the government's anticrisis measures helped to mitigate financial risks in the past few months, Russia's banking system isn't in the best shape. According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the share of bad loans will reach a peak of up to 17% in 2015 and the first half of 2016 before an expected decline later.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated that the current amount of international reserves of around $360 billion looks sufficient, though it needs to be gradually boosted to $500 billion to cover possibly massive capital flight given than global capital markets remain shut for Russian borrowers.\par \par \uc2 As Russia drained more than $150 billion in net capital outflow in 2014, when the West imposed sanctions against Moscow over its role in the Ukrainian crisis, Russia's gold and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell drastically. To avoid a deeper financial and economic crisis, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited the ruble's depreciation throughout 2014 by selling billions of dollars of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves from some $510 billion in late 2014 to their lowest levels since 2009.\par \par \uc2 After Western sanctions cut off Russia from global capital markets, the Bank of Russia also used reserves to provide foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the financial system, injecting $36 billion as of June 9. This helped to compensate for the lack of borrowing opportunities during the peak payments on foreign debt between October and April, the Bank of Russia said.\par \par \uc2 "Generally, rather good conditions of external trade balance and budget, a low level of state debt, an absence of excessive dependence of nonfinancial companies on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  funding, and a substantial stock of international reserves allow Russia to retain rather high immunity to possible imbalance on global financial markets," the bank said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said that despite the downgrade of Russia's sovereign rating below investment grade in early 2015, the country's treasury bonds, known as OFZs, are still in demand among foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Russian officials have recently been playing down the scale of the economic and financial crisis that hit the oil-dependent economy following its annexation of Crimea from Ukraine. While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  report is similar to recent government rhetoric saying the worst of the crisis is over, Russia's economy has slid into recession and is on track to contract by around 3% this year, suffering from double-digit inflation above 15%. The ruble's volatility, among the most notable movements on the global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over the past year, also remains a conundrum for Russia's financial system. Its volatility has been killing any investment activity, one of the key economic growth drivers, as the unpredictable exchange rate makes it difficult to do business.\par \par \uc2 Herman Gref\uc2 , the head of Russia's largest lender, \uc2 Sberbank\uc2 , said in late March--the period addressed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its report--that the worst for the country's banking system was clearly not over and that the bad debt issues would remain for a while.\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrey Ostroukh at andrey.ostroukh@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 23, 2015 05:14 ET (09:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150623eb6n000qi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
